add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Sustained Versus Nonsustained Ventricular Tachycardia',
 'Telemetry records monomorphic VT at 190/min. At 18 seconds the patient develops severe hypotension, and synchronized cardioversion terminates the rhythm. Which classification is appropriate?',
 'Sustained VT because hemodynamic compromise required intervention before 30 seconds',[
 ('Nonsustained VT because the recording lasted less than 30 seconds','A self-terminating ventricular run below thirty seconds can be nonsustained VT. Intervention for hemodynamic compromise is a separate sustained-VT criterion, so observed duration alone misclassifies this episode.'),
 ('Nonsustained VT because an electrical shock restored sinus rhythm','Successful treatment describes the outcome, not the initial rhythm category. A shock terminating a compromised ventricular rhythm does not convert it into a spontaneously terminating run.'),
 ('Ventricular fibrillation because hypotension developed during the episode','VF lacks organized repetitive ventricular complexes. Monomorphic VT can cause profound hypotension while remaining electrically organized; hemodynamic severity does not establish fibrillation.')],
 [S('Duration and Necessary Intervention Both Matter','**Sustained VT lasts at least 30 seconds or requires earlier intervention because of hemodynamic compromise.** Nonsustained VT consists of consecutive ventricular beats that terminate spontaneously before that duration boundary.',
    'ESC terminology defines VT as at least three consecutive ventricular beats at a rate above 100/min. Describe rate, morphology, duration, symptoms and termination rather than using the word run without these details.',
    'The early shock here interrupts observation, so the fact that only eighteen seconds were recorded does not demonstrate spontaneous self-termination. The documented deterioration and need for treatment establish the relevant exception.'),
  S('Classification Does Not Replace Immediate Assessment','**Treat attributable instability promptly while documenting the rhythm when feasible.** A pulse with unstable monomorphic VT calls for synchronized cardioversion; loss of pulse changes the resuscitation pathway.',
    'Following stabilization, obtain a twelve-lead ECG, assess electrolytes and potential drug effects, and investigate myocardial ischemia or structural disease according to the presentation. Review the preconversion tracing rather than relying solely on a monitor label.',
    'Nonsustained VT can still be clinically important in a patient with syncope, scar or inherited disease. Conversely, one sustained episode does not supply every fact needed for permanent device selection: assess reversible contributors, substrate, recurrence risk and expected clinical benefit. Electrical tolerance varies with rate, ventricular function and filling, so a relatively slow VT can cause shock and a faster episode can initially be tolerated. Communicate the intervention criterion clearly in the record; an automated duration-only report can otherwise understate a serious event.')],['ESC2022','ALS2025'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'AV Dissociation and Capture During Wide-Complex Tachycardia',
 'During a regular wide-complex tachycardia at 165/min, atrial deflections continue independently at 78/min. An occasional narrow complex matching the baseline sinus QRS appears within the tachycardia. Which mechanism is most strongly supported?',
 'Ventricular tachycardia with intermittent sinus capture',[
 ('Orthodromic AVRT with bundle-branch aberrancy','AVRT requires coordinated atrial and ventricular participation in its reentrant loop. Independent slower atrial activity and an intervening sinus-capture complex oppose that ordinary circuit.'),
 ('Focal atrial tachycardia conducted with fixed bundle-branch block','An atrial driver would need to account for the faster ventricular activation. The observed atrial rate is slower and independent, while a sinus impulse occasionally captures the ventricle.'),
 ('Antidromic AVRT with variable pre-excitation','Antidromic AVRT produces wide ventricular activation through an accessory pathway, but ordinary AVRT requires atrial participation. Slower dissociated atrial activity and sinus capture instead support an independent ventricular rhythm.')],
 [S('Look for Independent Atrial and Ventricular Activation','**AV dissociation during a wide-complex tachycardia strongly supports VT.** Atrial depolarizations march through at a different rate without a consistent relationship to the ventricular complexes.',
    'A capture beat occurs when a supraventricular impulse reaches the ventricle when conduction and myocardial refractoriness permit, producing a relatively normal complex within the ventricular rhythm. A fusion beat combines supraventricular and ventricular activation and has an intermediate morphology.',
    'Confirm that a narrow deflection is a genuine capture complex rather than artefact or a change in aberrancy. Compare with baseline QRS morphology and inspect atrial timing across multiple leads.'),
  S('Use Positive Signs Without Overinterpreting Their Absence','**Absence of visible dissociation, capture or fusion does not exclude VT.** Retrograde ventriculoatrial conduction can produce an apparent one-to-one association, and atrial activity may be buried in the QRS or T wave.',
    'Integrate prior MI or cardiomyopathy, baseline ECG, QRS morphology and the overall clinical setting. Surface algorithms can support diagnosis but do not justify assuming SVT whenever one criterion is absent.',
    'Rare AV nodal or junctional mechanisms can exhibit atrial dissociation, so interpret the entire tracing and seek expert electrophysiological assessment when needed. The combination here, including a sinus-capture complex, is particularly supportive of VT. Maintain a treatment strategy compatible with VT while uncertainty remains: empirical verapamil in an undifferentiated wide rhythm can cause collapse. Hemodynamic stability permits assessment but does not establish supraventricular origin. If attributable instability develops, prioritize electrical treatment rather than waiting for every diagnostic sign to appear.')],['ESC2022','ALS2025'])

R['SVT2019']=('ESC SVT guideline: wide-complex differential, 2019','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehz467')
add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Morphological Criteria in Regular Wide-Complex Tachycardia',
 'A patient with previous MI has regular wide-complex tachycardia. No RS complex is present in any precordial lead. Atrial activity cannot be confidently identified. Which interpretation is best?',
 'The morphology supports VT; lack of visible AV dissociation does not negate that evidence',[
 ('The tracing favors SVT because atrial dissociation has not been demonstrated','Visible dissociation strongly supports VT, but many VTs have concealed atrial activity or retrograde conduction. Its absence is not a positive criterion establishing supraventricular origin.'),
 ('The tracing favors SVT because an RS-interval measurement is unavailable','In the Brugada sequence, absence of RS complexes in all precordial leads itself supports VT. Measuring an RS interval is the next step when an RS complex exists, not a prerequisite for using the first criterion.'),
 ('The absence of RS complexes establishes antidromic AVRT through an accessory pathway','Pre-excited tachycardia can mimic ventricular-origin morphology, so this remains a differential. Absence of precordial RS complexes does not establish accessory-pathway participation, particularly in a patient with an infarct substrate.')],
 [S('Apply the Sequence to the Actual Tracing','**Absence of an RS complex in every precordial lead is a VT criterion in the Brugada algorithm for regular wide-complex tachycardia.** If an RS complex is present, a longest precordial RS interval greater than 100 ms is another supporting criterion.',
    'The sequence then considers AV dissociation and morphology in selected precordial leads. A negative earlier step means proceed through the assessment; it does not establish SVT by itself.',
    'Concordance means that precordial complexes share the same predominant polarity. Review the complete complexes rather than equating any prominent R wave with concordance.'),
  S('Recognize the Limits of Surface Algorithms','**ECG algorithms provide probabilistic evidence and must be interpreted with substrate and baseline conduction.** Pre-excitation, antiarrhythmic effects, electrolyte disturbance and unusual ventricular exit sites can complicate interpretation.',
    'A prior infarction increases concern for scar-related VT. Compare the tachycardia with a previous sinus tracing, and seek capture, fusion or atrial dissociation without requiring them all to be visible.',
    'Vereckei approaches use additional features such as initial aVR activation and relative initial versus terminal ventricular activation velocity; they are alternative structured assessments rather than a guarantee of perfect discrimination. An initial R in aVR can support ventricular origin, but no isolated sign should substitute for inspection of the full rhythm. Manage uncertain wide-complex tachycardia with a VT-compatible strategy and rescue readiness. Do not use preserved blood pressure or absent visible atrial dissociation to justify empirical verapamil. Document a twelve-lead ECG when feasible, but deterioration requires prompt electrical treatment rather than completion of a diagnostic checklist.')],['ESC2022','SVT2019'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Torsades Versus Polymorphic VT With a Normal QT',
 'Two monitored patients develop polymorphic VT. Between episodes, patient X has QTc 560 ms after a QT-prolonging drug and a pause preceding onset; patient Y has QTc 420 ms with acute ischemic chest pain. Which interpretation is most appropriate?',
 'X has a torsades phenotype; Y requires assessment for ischemia-associated polymorphic VT rather than automatic torsades labelling',[
 ('Both have torsades because beat-to-beat QRS morphology varies','Polymorphic morphology describes ventricular activation, but torsades specifically occurs in the setting of prolonged repolarization. Normal-QT polymorphic VT has a different etiological and treatment assessment.'),
 ('X most likely has ischemic polymorphic VT, while Y has drug-induced torsades','The supplied interepisode QT and pause-dependent onset support acquired long-QT torsades in X. Acute ischemic symptoms with a normal QT support a different mechanism in Y rather than the reversed assignment.'),
 ('Both most likely have CPVT because each has a polymorphic rhythm','CPVT is considered with adrenergically provoked bidirectional or polymorphic VT, typically with a normal resting ECG and no explanatory structural disease. Neither polymorphism alone nor these two supplied triggers establishes that inherited syndrome.')],
 [S('Measure Repolarization Outside the Ventricular Run','**Torsades de pointes is polymorphic VT associated with prolonged QT in the intervening rhythm.** The apparent twisting pattern alone is insufficient to identify its mechanism.',
    'Review a reliable baseline or interepisode ECG, the rate, drugs and electrolytes. QT interpretation is difficult during a rapid ventricular run, and broad QRS complexes or unusual T–U morphology can complicate automated measurements.',
    'A short–long–short sequence or pause-dependent onset supports acquired long-QT torsades. Assess bradycardia, potassium and magnesium depletion, drug interactions and impaired drug clearance.'),
  S('The Distinction Changes Recurrence Treatment','**Sustained polymorphic VT requires an immediate unsynchronized shock because reliable synchronization is difficult.** Loss of pulse requires the shockable-arrest pathway; an apparently tolerable brief run still warrants urgent monitoring and cause assessment.',
    'For recurrent long-QT torsades, magnesium and correction of the cause are important; remove QT-prolonging agents and correct electrolyte deficiency. Selected recurrent acquired pause-dependent cases may require rate acceleration through pacing or isoproterenol under specialist monitoring.',
    'With a normal QT, investigate ischemia and other substrates; urgent coronary assessment and appropriate antiarrhythmic therapy can be consequential. AHA 2025 does not recommend routine magnesium simply for normal-QT polymorphic VT. Magnesium remains appropriate for documented deficiency, which is a separate indication. Do not extrapolate isoproterenol used in acquired bradycardia-related torsades to congenital long-QT disease or active ischemia without reviewing the mechanism. A treatment that is helpful in one polymorphic-rhythm phenotype can be ineffective or harmful in another.')],['ESC2022','ALS2025'])

R['DIGOXIN']=('Digoxin prescribing information, 2025','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/009330s040lbl.pdf')
add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Bidirectional Ventricular Tachycardia in Clinical Context',
 'An older patient taking digoxin develops vomiting, new renal impairment and ventricular tachycardia with alternating frontal QRS axis on successive beats. The resting QT before the episode was not prolonged. Which cause is most likely?',
 'Digoxin toxicity',[
 ('Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia','CPVT can produce bidirectional VT, usually during adrenergic stress in a patient without explanatory structural disease. New renal impairment, digoxin exposure and gastrointestinal symptoms make an acquired toxic mechanism more likely here.'),
 ('Acquired long-QT torsades de pointes','Drug-induced torsades is associated with prolonged repolarization and commonly pause-dependent polymorphic VT. Alternation between two ventricular axes with a previously normal QT and this exposure history favors digoxin toxicity.'),
 ('Scar-related monomorphic ventricular tachycardia','Scar reentry is important in structural heart disease and generally produces a stable monomorphic pattern during a given episode. Beat-to-beat axis alternation and systemic digoxin-toxicity clues require a different explanation.')],
 [S('A Pattern Can Have More Than One Cause','**Bidirectional VT shows beat-to-beat alternation of the frontal QRS axis.** Important associations include digoxin toxicity, CPVT, Andersen–Tawil syndrome and occasionally acute myocardial disease.',
    'Digoxin-related sodium–potassium ATPase inhibition can cause intracellular calcium overload and triggered activity. Renal impairment reduces clearance and makes a previously tolerated chronic regimen hazardous.',
    'Ask about dosing changes, interacting medicines and gastrointestinal or neurological symptoms. A new drug interaction can raise exposure without a newly increased digoxin prescription.'),
  S('Treat the Toxic Syndrome Rather Than the ECG Label Alone','**Stop digoxin and assess rhythm, perfusion, renal function and electrolytes urgently.** Life-threatening ventricular arrhythmia in suspected digoxin toxicity warrants digoxin immune Fab and specialist toxicology/cardiology support.',
    'Measure a properly timed concentration, generally at least six hours after a dose, but do not postpone antidotal treatment of life-threatening toxicity to await a level. Clinical toxicity can occur at concentrations that appear acceptable in a vulnerable patient.',
    'Potassium abnormalities require careful interpretation: depletion can increase susceptibility, while substantial toxicity can produce hyperkalemia. Monitor potassium closely after Fab because it may fall as the pharmacological effect reverses. Routine total digoxin assays become difficult to interpret after Fab because bound drug contributes to the measured result. Correct the precipitating renal or interaction problem and reassess the need for future treatment. A younger patient with exertional syncope and an otherwise normal evaluation would instead need assessment for CPVT; the same morphology does not establish the same cause in every clinical setting.')],['ESC2022','DIGOXIN'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Incidental NSVT With a High-Risk Symptom',
 'A 52-year-old has an incidental eight-beat ventricular run on ambulatory monitoring. On review, the patient reports recent abrupt loss of consciousness while walking, without a vagal prodrome. The office ECG and examination do not establish a cause. Which next approach is most appropriate?',
 'Prompt evaluation for cardiac syncope and underlying disease, with consideration of monitored admission',[
 ('Reassure and discharge because the run was shorter than thirty seconds','Short self-terminating runs can be low risk after an appropriate normal evaluation. Recent unexplained syncope suspicious for an arrhythmic cause is a red flag that changes the evaluation despite the short recording.'),
 ('Start an antiarrhythmic to suppress the recorded run before investigating the syncope','Treatment can be needed for selected symptomatic or high-risk ventricular arrhythmias, but suppression of one recorded run does not establish the syncope mechanism or identify structural and inherited disease.'),
 ('Implant an ICD solely because any NSVT was documented','An ICD may become indicated after substrate and risk assessment. A brief run alone does not establish a device indication, and the work-up must identify the actual disease and relationship to symptoms.')],
 [S('Symptoms and Substrate Determine Urgency','**Incidental NSVT requires contextual evaluation rather than classification by duration alone.** ESC 2022 identifies recent syncope suspicious for cardiac origin as a high-risk feature that may prompt admission.',
    'Clarify posture, exertion, prodrome, injury, witness observations and recovery. Abrupt syncope without a typical vagal prodrome raises concern, but the recorded run is not automatically proven to be the cause.',
    'Review family history of premature sudden death, unexplained drowning or seizure-like episodes, and personal ischemic, cardiomyopathic and drug history. A normal examination does not exclude an arrhythmogenic substrate.'),
  S('Build a Directed Initial Investigation','**A twelve-lead ECG, echocardiography and ambulatory rhythm characterization form the initial assessment.** Quantify ventricular ectopy and NSVT burden and assess morphology, rate, coupling and relation to activity.',
    'Check relevant electrolytes and reversible contributors. Investigate coronary disease according to symptoms and risk profile, and use CMR when cardiomyopathy or inflammatory disease remains suspected despite an unrevealing first assessment.',
    'Exercise testing can document adrenergic arrhythmias when clinically appropriate, but unstable symptoms or concern for significant disease require a supervised risk-based plan rather than an unsupervised exercise challenge. Typical outflow-tract or fascicular morphology with a normal ECG and echo and negative family history can support an idiopathic diagnosis in another patient. Multifocality, abnormal baseline ECG, ventricular dysfunction or exercise-related polymorphic arrhythmias argue for deeper assessment. Long-term monitoring or electrophysiological testing is selected according to the emerging substrate and syncope question. Device treatment follows a defined risk indication, not the incidental-monitor label alone; reassess if new symptoms or disease evidence appears.')],['ESC2022'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'CMR in Ventricular Arrhythmia Despite a Normal Echocardiogram',
 'A 58-year-old has recurrent ventricular arrhythmia with more than one morphology and an episode of unexplained syncope. TTE shows normal chamber dimensions and preserved EF. Which investigation best assesses an occult myocardial substrate?',
 'Cardiac magnetic resonance with appropriate tissue characterization',[
 ('Extended ambulatory ECG monitoring','Monitoring quantifies burden and can correlate symptoms with rhythm, but it does not characterize myocardial scar or inflammation. Further monitoring can complement rather than replace imaging in this atypical presentation.'),
 ('Supervised exercise ECG testing','Exercise testing can assess exertional arrhythmia and selected ischemic questions. It cannot provide the myocardial tissue characterization needed to investigate an occult cardiomyopathic or inflammatory substrate.'),
 ('Invasive electrophysiological study','EPS can define mechanisms, inducibility or an ablation target in selected settings. It does not replace tissue characterization when the clinical question is occult myocardial disease after a normal echo.')],
 [S('An Apparently Normal Heart Is Not Yet a Proven Idiopathic Substrate','**ESC 2022 advises considering CMR when PVCs or VT have an atypical presentation despite a normal echocardiogram.** Preserved global EF does not exclude a localized scar or early cardiomyopathy.',
    'Review the clinical history, family history, baseline ECG and ventricular-arrhythmia morphology. Multiple morphologies, concerning syncope or an unusual presumed origin justify a deeper etiological assessment.',
    'Typical idiopathic outflow-tract or fascicular arrhythmia with otherwise reassuring findings is a different setting. Avoid labelling a rhythm idiopathic solely because the first structural test is normal.'),
  S('Interpret Tissue Findings With the Whole Phenotype','**CMR assesses ventricular volumes and regional function and can identify late gadolinium enhancement suggesting myocardial injury or fibrosis.** Appropriate sequences can also support evaluation of active inflammation.',
    'An ischemic-pattern scar and a nonischemic distribution lead to different diagnostic considerations. Integrate coronary assessment, inflammatory evaluation and genetic counseling when the overall findings justify them.',
    'LGE is not a histological diagnosis by itself, and imaging artefact or nonspecific findings require experienced interpretation. Renal status, implanted hardware and the proposed contrast protocol affect feasibility. A normal study lowers the likelihood of detectable structural disease but does not exclude a primary electrical syndrome or every microscopic abnormality. Results can change ablation planning and disease-specific sudden-death assessment, but finding a small scar does not independently establish a universal ICD indication. Continue symptom and rhythm assessment while the etiological work-up proceeds; concerning syncope should not be dismissed because EF remains normal. Reassess the diagnosis if new ventricular dysfunction, ECG changes or family information emerges.')],['ESC2022'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Systematic Evaluation After Unexplained Resuscitated VF',
 'A 34-year-old survives documented VF. Electrolytes and toxicology assessment reveal no explanatory abnormality, coronary imaging shows no culprit disease, and initial echo shows no major structural lesion. Which next approach is most appropriate before diagnosing idiopathic VF?',
 'Continue structured evaluation with repeat ECG assessment, CMR and phenotype-directed provocative and family evaluation',[
 ('Diagnose idiopathic VF from the normal coronary and echocardiographic studies','These tests exclude important causes but do not complete the assessment for concealed cardiomyopathy or inherited electrical disease. Idiopathic VF is an exclusion diagnosis after an appropriate comprehensive evaluation.'),
 ('Use an immediate broad genetic panel as the sole remaining investigation','Genetic testing can be informative when guided by phenotype and counseling. An unselected panel does not replace clinical testing and can produce uncertain variants that do not establish the cause.'),
 ('Use inducibility at EPS as the sole test to distinguish inherited from idiopathic VF','EPS has selected indications, but inducibility does not comprehensively distinguish channelopathy, occult structural disease and idiopathic VF. The evaluation requires complementary clinical, electrical and imaging evidence.')],
 [S('Preserve Evidence and Reassess After Recovery','**A survivor of unexplained cardiac arrest needs a structured search for structural, coronary, electrical, metabolic and toxic causes.** Retrieve the original rhythm, circumstances, AED or monitor recordings and relevant medication history.',
    'Repeat ECGs after stabilization because post-arrest physiology, drugs and temperature can transiently alter repolarization. ESC guidance advises reassessing QT several days after arrest rather than diagnosing congenital long QT from one immediate tracing.',
    'Reassess ventricular function when early dysfunction could represent stunning. Normalization can clarify the substrate, but it does not make documented VF irrelevant or terminate the etiological work-up.'),
  S('Use Complementary Tests and Appropriate Family Assessment','**CMR can reveal concealed cardiomyopathy, scar or inflammation missed by the first echocardiogram.** Review standard and high right-precordial ECG leads when Brugada syndrome is suspected.',
    'Exercise testing can assess adrenergically triggered arrhythmia; selected sodium-channel-blocker or coronary-spasm testing requires an experienced protocol and a relevant clinical question. Provocative testing is not an interchangeable battery without indications or interpretation safeguards.',
    'Obtain a detailed family history and arrange phenotype-directed clinical and genetic evaluation with counseling. A variant of uncertain significance must not be treated as a confirmed causal diagnosis or used alone for predictive testing in relatives. Idiopathic VF is considered only after a suitable exclusion process, and follow-up may reveal a phenotype not apparent initially. Secondary-prevention protection and etiological testing are related but separate decisions: an incomplete investigation does not justify leaving a survivor unprotected while awaiting a molecular answer. Review the possibility of a truly reversible cause, expected clinical benefit and the eventual ICD strategy with the specialist team.')],['ESC2022'])

R['ARREST2025']=('AHA adult cardiac arrest algorithm, 2025','https://cpr.heart.org/-/media/CPR-Files/CPR-Guidelines-Files/2025-Algorithms/Algorithm-ACLS-CA-250527.pdf')
R['TACHY2025']=('AHA tachyarrhythmia with pulse algorithm, 2025','https://cpr.heart.org/-/media/CPR-Files/CPR-Guidelines-Files/2025-Algorithms/Algorithm-ACLS-Tachycardia-250514.pdf')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Immediate Treatment of Pulseless Ventricular Tachycardia',
 'A monitored adult suddenly becomes unresponsive with absent normal breathing and no definite pulse on a rapid check. The monitor shows rapid regular wide-complex tachycardia, and a defibrillator is ready. Which immediate strategy is appropriate?',
 'Deliver unsynchronized defibrillation and resume high-quality CPR immediately',[
 ('Deliver synchronized cardioversion and reassess the pulse before starting compressions','Synchronized cardioversion is used for appropriate unstable tachyarrhythmia with a pulse. Pulseless VT belongs to the cardiac-arrest pathway; synchronization and a prolonged postshock pulse check can delay lifesaving treatment.'),
 ('Give IV amiodarone before the first shock','Amiodarone or lidocaine can be considered for shock-refractory VF/pVT. Drug preparation must not delay an available first defibrillation shock in this newly recognized shockable arrest.'),
 ('Give epinephrine before the first shock and await rhythm conversion','Epinephrine supports resuscitation when indicated but does not replace defibrillation. In the AHA shockable pathway, early shock attempts take priority over delaying treatment for a vasopressor response.')],
 [S('Pulse Status Determines the Pathway','**VF and pulseless VT are shockable cardiac-arrest rhythms.** Start high-quality CPR while the defibrillator is prepared and deliver an unsynchronized shock as soon as available.',
    'A rapid organized ventricular tracing does not establish effective circulation. Assess responsiveness, breathing and pulse promptly; do not spend a prolonged interval searching for a pulse when arrest is otherwise evident.',
    'Use the manufacturer-recommended biphasic energy, with the maximum available dose if the recommendation is unknown in the AHA arrest algorithm. Subsequent shocks use an equivalent or higher setting according to the device and protocol.'),
  S('Resume Perfusion Support Rather Than Watching the Monitor','**Resume CPR immediately after the shock, normally for two minutes before the scheduled rhythm assessment.** Minimize interruptions, ensure effective compressions and ventilation, and establish IV access or IO access when appropriate.',
    'The AHA 2025 shockable algorithm introduces epinephrine during CPR after the second shock and amiodarone or lidocaine after the third shock if VF/pVT persists. Epinephrine is 1 mg IV/IO every 3–5 minutes; arrest-dose amiodarone is 300 mg, with a subsequent 150-mg dose when indicated.',
    'These arrest regimens differ from slow loading infusions for a perfusing tachycardia. Search for reversible causes such as hypoxia, electrolyte disturbance, toxins and coronary thrombosis while maintaining the shock/CPR sequence. A visible postshock rhythm does not by itself prove return of circulation; assess at the appropriate pause or earlier if convincing signs of ROSC develop. After ROSC, address oxygenation, ventilation, hemodynamics and the cause of arrest. The immediate question concerns restoring circulation, not selecting long-term antiarrhythmic suppression or an ICD before resuscitation is complete.')],['ARREST2025','ALS2025'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Unstable Monomorphic VT With a Pulse',
 'An adult has regular monomorphic VT at 180/min with a palpable pulse. BP falls to 64/38 mmHg, with confusion and acute pulmonary edema attributable to the rhythm. Pads are attached. Which next intervention is most appropriate?',
 'Prompt synchronized cardioversion with concurrent supportive care',[
 ('Complete a procainamide infusion before considering electrical treatment','Procainamide can treat selected tolerated monomorphic VT with monitoring. Severe attributable hypotension and pulmonary edema require urgent electrical treatment rather than waiting for an infusion that may worsen pressure.'),
 ('Give adenosine as the required diagnostic step before cardioversion','Adenosine has a restricted role in stable regular monomorphic wide-complex tachycardia when mechanism is uncertain. It is not a prerequisite for treating this documented unstable ventricular rhythm.'),
 ('Deliver an immediate unsynchronized shock','Unsynchronized shock is required for pulseless VT/VF and sustained polymorphic VT, and may be necessary if synchronization cannot be achieved during critical deterioration. With a pulse, monomorphic complexes and reliable synchronization available, synchronized treatment is preferred.')],
 [S('Treat the Hemodynamic Consequence Immediately','**Monomorphic VT causing shock, altered mentation, ischemic discomfort or acute HF requires prompt synchronized cardioversion.** A palpable pulse distinguishes this presentation from pulseless VT but does not make delay safe.',
    'Support airway, ventilation and circulation, and prepare electrical treatment concurrently. Sedation is appropriate when feasible, but profound deterioration must not be prolonged while completing an elective sedation process.',
    'Confirm that the rhythm is materially causing compromise. Compensatory sinus tachycardia in shock requires treating the driver; the documented ventricular rhythm and temporal deterioration here support direct rhythm treatment.'),
  S('Use Synchronization Correctly and Reassess','**Verify synchronization markers on the QRS complexes before the shock.** Synchronization reduces the risk of delivering energy during vulnerable repolarization.',
    'Use the device-recommended setting or relevant rhythm-specific guidance; AHA 2025 specifies the maximum energy setting when the recommendation is unknown. Recheck synchronization after each attempt because devices may reset the mode.',
    'If synchronization is impossible and the patient is critically deteriorating, do not allow an equipment limitation to cause a fatal delay; deliver an appropriate unsynchronized shock. Reassess pulse and perfusion after treatment and follow the arrest pathway immediately if the pulse is lost. After stabilization, investigate ischemia, scar, electrolyte disturbance and drug effects and plan prevention of recurrence. Successful cardioversion ends the episode but does not establish that the substrate is benign or that long-term device protection is unnecessary. Distinguish this rescue decision from the separate choice of antiarrhythmic or ablation in a patient whose monomorphic VT is genuinely well tolerated.')],['TACHY2025','ALS2025','ESC2022'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Electrical Treatment of Sustained Polymorphic VT',
 'An adult develops sustained polymorphic VT with rapidly varying QRS configuration and severe circulatory compromise. A pulse is initially detectable, but synchronization markers cannot reliably track the changing complexes. Which immediate intervention is appropriate?',
 'Deliver an immediate high-energy unsynchronized shock',[
 ('Continue adjusting synchronization until every changing complex is reliably marked','Synchronization is useful for monomorphic VT with a pulse. Sustained polymorphic VT cannot reliably be synchronized, and repeated attempts can dangerously delay the indicated unsynchronized shock.'),
 ('Give magnesium and wait for the rhythm to terminate before electrical treatment','Magnesium helps prevent recurrences of long-QT torsades, but it does not replace immediate shock treatment of sustained polymorphic VT. The QT mechanism is assessed after or alongside rescue.'),
 ('Give an amiodarone infusion before deciding whether a shock is necessary','Antiarrhythmic selection can matter for recurrence, particularly normal-QT polymorphic VT. Sustained polymorphic VT with compromise requires immediate electrical treatment, and amiodarone is not an appropriate default for acquired long-QT torsades.')],
 [S('Shock Mode Depends on Rhythm Organization','**AHA 2025 recommends immediate unsynchronized shock for sustained polymorphic VT.** Beat-to-beat changes prevent reliable timing of a synchronized shock to a stable QRS complex.',
    'A detectable pulse does not convert sustained polymorphic VT into a reliably synchronizable rhythm. Use a high-energy defibrillation setting according to the device and protocol rather than waiting for a prolonged synchronization attempt.',
    'Assess pulse and circulation promptly after treatment. If pulseless arrest is present, follow the VF/pVT resuscitation pathway with immediate CPR and repeated shock/CPR cycles as indicated.'),
  S('Prevent Recurrence According to the Interepisode QT','**Electrical rescue terminates the current rhythm but does not identify or remove its cause.** Examine a reliable interepisode ECG for QT prolongation and review ischemic symptoms, medications, electrolytes and preceding pauses.',
    'Long-QT torsades calls for magnesium, correction of electrolyte abnormalities and removal of precipitating drugs. Selected acquired pause-dependent cases can require rate acceleration with monitored pacing or isoproterenol.',
    'Normal-QT polymorphic VT frequently raises an ischemic concern and can require urgent coronary treatment and selected antiarrhythmics. Routine magnesium has not demonstrated benefit simply because a normal-QT ventricular rhythm is polymorphic, although a measured deficiency still requires correction. Do not add QT-prolonging therapy indiscriminately to a patient whose arrhythmia is driven by prolonged repolarization. Obtain specialist assistance and maintain defibrillation readiness because recurrent episodes may rapidly become pulseless. Brief self-terminating runs require urgent evaluation too, but they are distinct from the sustained rhythm in this stem; one should not wait for a sustained episode to end spontaneously on the assumption that previous short runs did so.')],['ALS2025','ESC2022'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Drug Selection for Tolerated Monomorphic VT',
 'A patient has sustained regular monomorphic VT at 158/min, BP 126/78 mmHg and normal mentation without congestion or ischemic discomfort. LVEF is 50%, renal function is normal, and baseline QT is not prolonged. With continuous ECG/BP monitoring and cardioversion available, which initial regimen follows the AHA 2025 stable wide-QRS tachycardia algorithm?',
 'Procainamide 20–50 mg/min, stopping for suppression, hypotension, QRS widening greater than 50%, or 17 mg/kg',[
 ('Amiodarone 300 mg by rapid IV push, followed by assessment for a further 150 mg','This uses the shock-refractory VF/pulseless-VT arrest bolus sequence. For tolerated VT with a pulse, the algorithm instead gives amiodarone 150 mg over ten minutes, with subsequent infusion as appropriate.'),
 ('Procainamide maintenance at 1–4 mg/min as the initial infusion, with loading deferred until recurrence','This is a maintenance rate after appropriate loading and reassessment. It is not the algorithm loading regimen for converting the ongoing sustained ventricular tachycardia.'),
 ('Amiodarone 150 mg over ten minutes with concurrent procainamide loading at 20–50 mg/min','Either agent can be considered individually in suitable patients, but concurrent loading is not the algorithm strategy. Combined conduction and repolarization effects increase hypotension and proarrhythmia; reassess and obtain expert guidance rather than routinely stacking drugs.')],
 [S('Tolerated VT Allows Assessment but Requires Active Treatment','**Monitored IV procainamide is an option for selected stable monomorphic VT.** Amiodarone or sotalol can also be considered in appropriate settings under AHA guidance; suitability depends on pressure, ventricular function, QT, renal function and interactions.',
    'Obtain a twelve-lead ECG and review the baseline rhythm, but maintain defibrillator readiness. A patient can deteriorate while appearing initially comfortable, and new attributable instability changes the priority to electrical treatment.',
    'The absence of severe HF, acute MI, renal failure and prolonged QT supports considering procainamide here. It does not mean that these safety checks can be omitted in another patient.'),
  S('Use an Explicit Infusion and Stopping Protocol','**The AHA tachyarrhythmia algorithm uses procainamide 20–50 mg/min until suppression or a stopping limit.** Stop for hypotension, QRS widening greater than 50% or a cumulative 17 mg/kg; a maintenance infusion of 1–4 mg/min may follow when appropriate.',
    'Avoid procainamide in prolonged QT or significant HF; ESC also cautions against use in acute MI and end-stage renal disease. Monitor the evolving QRS, QT, rhythm and pressure rather than treating the maximum dose as a target to reach.',
    'Do not routinely combine multiple repolarization-prolonging antiarrhythmics, because additive effects can cause proarrhythmia and hypotension. Synchronized cardioversion remains appropriate if drugs are ineffective or contraindicated, even before profound shock develops. The PROCAMIO comparison supports procainamide in its selected tolerated wide-complex population but does not prove universal superiority across arrest, decompensated HF or every VT mechanism. After conversion, investigate the substrate and arrange a recurrence-prevention strategy; acute suppression does not replace disease-specific ablation or ICD assessment when indicated.')],['ALS2025','TACHY2025','ESC2022'])

R['PROCAMIO']=('PROCAMIO randomized trial, online 2016; print 2017','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehw230')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Interpreting the PROCAMIO Trial',
 'When discussing drug conversion of tolerated regular wide-complex tachycardia, which statement accurately describes PROCAMIO?',
 'Procainamide produced fewer major acute cardiac adverse events and more terminations within 40 minutes than amiodarone in the studied population',[
 ('Procainamide improved long-term survival compared with amiodarone after resuscitated VF','The trial assessed short-term treatment of tolerated monomorphic wide-complex tachycardia, not long-term survival after VF arrest. That population and endpoint require different evidence.'),
 ('Amiodarone produced more terminations within 40 minutes, while procainamide caused fewer adverse events','The safety direction is consistent with the trial, but the termination result is reversed. Procainamide had the higher acute termination proportion as well as fewer major cardiac adverse events.'),
 ('Procainamide and amiodarone were compared with immediate cardioversion as a third randomized strategy','The randomized comparison was between two drug regimens. Electrical cardioversion remained a rescue option but was not a third randomized treatment arm.')],
 [S('Know What Was Actually Randomized','**PROCAMIO compared IV procainamide with IV amiodarone for hemodynamically tolerated regular monomorphic wide-complex tachycardia, presumed predominantly VT.** It was a small, open-label randomized study, with 62 patients included in the main analysis.',
    'The regimens were procainamide 10 mg/kg over 20 minutes and amiodarone 5 mg/kg over 20 minutes. These trial regimens should be distinguished from the specific bolus or infusion schedules in resuscitation algorithms.',
    'The study does not justify delaying cardioversion in shock or extrapolating the same drug balance to pulseless arrest, severe decompensated HF or a substantially different arrhythmia.'),
  S('Separate Acute Efficacy From Long-Term Outcomes','**Major cardiac adverse events during the initial 40-minute period occurred in approximately 9% with procainamide versus 41% with amiodarone.** Tachycardia terminated within 40 minutes in 67% versus 38%, respectively.',
    'The adverse-event outcome included clinically important deterioration, particularly hypotension. Lower acute toxicity in this comparison does not make procainamide safe without monitoring or remove its QT, HF and renal restrictions.',
    'The sample size, open-label design and selected tolerated presentation limit generalization. A superiority finding for an acute termination endpoint does not establish better long-term survival, freedom from recurrence or avoidance of an ICD. Amiodarone remains an option in other appropriate settings and should not be described as ineffective from this single comparison. Use the evidence to inform a patient-specific monitored strategy, maintaining synchronized cardioversion readiness and avoiding routine stacking of antiarrhythmics. After conversion, pursue the underlying substrate and prevention plan rather than assuming that a successful infusion has treated myocardial scar or its subsequent risk.')],['PROCAMIO','ESC2022','ALS2025'],style='direct',
 table=(['PROCAMIO outcome within 40 minutes','Procainamide','Amiodarone'],[
 ['Major cardiac adverse events','9%','41%'],['Tachycardia termination','67%','38%']]))

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Procainamide Infusion Stopping Criteria',
 'During monitored procainamide treatment of tolerated monomorphic VT, QRS duration increases from 150 to 235 ms. BP remains adequate and the cumulative dose is only 9 mg/kg. Which action best follows the AHA infusion limits?',
 'Stop the infusion because QRS widening exceeds the specified safety limit',[
 ('Continue to 17 mg/kg because blood pressure is preserved','Seventeen mg/kg is a maximum dose, not an obligation to complete loading. Excessive QRS widening is an independent stopping criterion even before hypotension develops.'),
 ('Reduce the infusion rate and continue until conversion because the dose is below the maximum','Slowing infusion can be relevant to some tolerability problems, but the algorithm specifies stopping when QRS duration increases by more than fifty percent. Continuing exposure disregards that independent limit.'),
 ('Add amiodarone while continuing procainamide to improve the termination rate','Combining drugs can add hypotension and repolarization-related proarrhythmia. Failure to convert before a procainamide safety endpoint is reached calls for reassessment and an alternative strategy, not routine simultaneous loading.')],
 [S('Calculate the Change From the Starting QRS','**The QRS has widened by 85 ms, approximately 57% of its initial 150-ms duration.** The AHA algorithm stops procainamide when QRS duration increases by more than 50%, even if pressure remains acceptable.',
    'Compare like-for-like complexes during the same rhythm and verify the measurement rather than accepting artefact or a changed rhythm as drug-induced widening. Here the supplied measurements establish a qualifying increase.',
    'Additional stopping points are arrhythmia suppression, hypotension or the cumulative 17-mg/kg maximum. Each is sufficient; they are not conditions that must occur together.'),
  S('Reassess Treatment Rather Than Chasing the Maximum Dose','**Procainamide slows ventricular conduction through sodium-channel blockade and can widen QRS as exposure increases.** Its active metabolite also contributes repolarization effects, and impaired renal clearance increases accumulation risk.',
    'The AHA loading rate is 20–50 mg/min, with a maintenance infusion of 1–4 mg/min when appropriate after successful treatment. Avoid prolonged QT or significant HF, and review renal status, other drugs and the actual clinical substrate.',
    'Stop further loading here, maintain ECG and pressure surveillance and reassess pulse, perfusion and treatment options. Persistent VT may require synchronized cardioversion, especially if deterioration occurs or further drugs are unsuitable. Do not automatically restart at a lower rate merely because the QRS later narrows; review the cumulative exposure and overall strategy with expert support. Avoid stacking QT-prolonging agents without a justified specialist plan. Document both the starting and stopping measurements so a later clinician does not interpret an incomplete load as treatment abandonment rather than a safety-directed decision.')],['TACHY2025','ALS2025','ESC2022'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Amiodarone Dosing in Perfusing VT Versus Cardiac Arrest',
 'A patient has tolerated monomorphic VT with a pulse. After expert assessment, IV amiodarone is selected. Which initial regimen matches the AHA 2025 tachyarrhythmia-with-pulse algorithm?',
 '150 mg IV over 10 minutes with continuous monitoring',[
 ('300 mg IV as a rapid arrest-dose bolus','A 300-mg IV/IO bolus is the initial amiodarone regimen for shock-refractory VF/pVT during cardiac arrest. It should not be transferred unchanged to a perfusing patient, where rapid administration can cause marked hypotension.'),
 ('150 mg IV over two minutes as the standard perfusing-rhythm load','The supplied algorithm specifies a ten-minute initial infusion. Compressing that infusion into a rapid bolus changes the safety profile, especially when pressure and ventricular function are vulnerable.'),
 ('Start 1 mg/min without the initial loading infusion','One mg/min is the subsequent first-six-hour infusion in the algorithm. It is not the specified initial loading regimen when acute pharmacological conversion is selected.')],
 [S('Name the Clinical Pathway Before the Dose','**AHA 2025 lists amiodarone 150 mg over 10 minutes for stable wide-QRS tachycardia.** It can be repeated as needed if VT recurs, with a subsequent infusion of 1 mg/min for the first six hours.',
    'This is distinct from the cardiac-arrest regimen: initial 300 mg IV/IO, then 150 mg when indicated for shock-refractory VF/pVT. Drugs remain adjuncts to defibrillation and CPR in arrest rather than reasons to delay a shock.',
    'Lidocaine is an alternative arrest antiarrhythmic, initially 1–1.5 mg/kg IV/IO, with a subsequent 0.5–0.75 mg/kg dose under the algorithm. It is not evidence-based to treat every tolerated VT as requiring the arrest regimen of either agent.'),
  S('Monitor the Infusion and the Substrate','**Watch blood pressure, rhythm, conduction and QT during IV amiodarone.** Hypotension can result from infusion-related effects and the patient’s underlying disease; clinically important deterioration requires immediate reassessment.',
    'Amiodarone is not a universal choice for polymorphic VT. Acquired long-QT torsades requires a different strategy, and adding a repolarization-prolonging drug can worsen the substrate.',
    'Review previous antiarrhythmics and avoid indiscriminate combined loading with procainamide or sotalol. A stable pressure at the beginning of treatment does not guarantee continued tolerance. Synchronized cardioversion is appropriate when a monomorphic rhythm becomes unstable or drug treatment is ineffective or unsuitable. After the acute phase, check the ongoing indication and regimen rather than continuing an emergency infusion without a plan. Longer exposure requires attention to organ toxicity and interactions, including digoxin and warfarin. Successful conversion does not replace assessment for myocardial ischemia, scar, ablation or an independently indicated ICD. Document that a pulse was present when the regimen was selected, because confusing arrest and perfusing-rhythm doses is a preventable prescribing error.')],['TACHY2025','ARREST2025','ALS2025'],style='direct')

R['EHRA2018']=('EHRA/ESC antiarrhythmic clinical-use consensus, 2018','https://doi.org/10.1093/europace/eux373')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Magnesium and Trigger Correction in Acquired Torsades',
 'A patient taking a new QT-prolonging drug has repeated self-terminating polymorphic ventricular runs preceded by pauses. Between runs, QTc is 590 ms and potassium is 3.0 mmol/L. Serum magnesium is within the laboratory reference range. Which immediate recurrence-prevention strategy is most appropriate while defibrillation remains available?',
 'Give IV magnesium, stop the precipitating drug and correct potassium with monitoring',[
 ('Withhold magnesium because its serum concentration is normal','Magnesium can suppress long-QT torsades even without a low measured serum level. Normal serum magnesium does not remove this arrhythmia-specific treatment role.'),
 ('Stop the precipitating drug and begin an amiodarone infusion','Amiodarone has roles in selected normal-QT ventricular arrhythmias, but this is a prolonged-repolarization substrate. Removing the culprit is appropriate; adding another repolarization-prolonging agent is a poor choice for the acquired torsades mechanism.'),
 ('Give a rate-slowing beta blocker as the primary response to the pauses','Beta blockers are central in congenital long-QT treatment and selected adrenergic syndromes. In acquired pause-dependent torsades, further bradycardia can promote recurrence, so rate slowing is not the appropriate primary corrective strategy.')],
 [S('Treat Prolonged Repolarization and the Trigger Together','**Recurrent pause-dependent polymorphic VT with marked interepisode QT prolongation is an acquired torsades phenotype.** Stop the offending drug and review interacting medicines, renal clearance and other causes of prolonged repolarization.',
    'IV magnesium sulfate is used even if the serum magnesium level is normal. The EHRA clinical-use consensus describes 2 g IV as first-line treatment; administration rate and any repeat dosing depend on perfusion, renal status and the monitored emergency protocol.',
    'Correct potassium depletion and reassess magnesium and other electrolytes. Repletion toward high-normal potassium, approximately 4.5–5.0 mmol/L, may be considered with careful renal and ECG monitoring; the evidence for that exact target is less robust than the need to correct hypokalemia.'),
  S('Separate Recurrence Prevention From Electrical Rescue','**Sustained polymorphic VT requires an immediate unsynchronized shock.** Repeated short runs do not justify leaving the patient without continuous monitoring and immediate defibrillation capability.',
    'Magnesium can suppress arrhythmia without immediately normalizing QT. Continue surveillance until the trigger and repolarization abnormality have resolved rather than interpreting one quiet interval as complete recovery.',
    'Persistent acquired pause-dependent recurrence despite correction may require temporary pacing or selected isoproterenol to increase the underlying rate. This is different from the beta-blocker-centered approach to congenital long QT, and isoproterenol should not be extrapolated uncritically to active ischemia or inherited disease. Avoid adding other QT-prolonging drugs to an already vulnerable substrate. Document the suspected culprit, review future prescribing and consider an underlying inherited susceptibility if QT remains prolonged after recovery or family history is concerning. A normal magnesium concentration neither excludes torsades nor proves that additional magnesium is unnecessary in this specific clinical setting.')],['ALS2025','ESC2022','EHRA2018'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Recurrent Acquired Pause-Dependent Torsades',
 'An adult has recurrent pause-triggered torsades after a QT-prolonging drug. The drug has been stopped, potassium corrected and IV magnesium given, but episodes continue during sinus bradycardia at 38/min. There is no active ischemia or known congenital long-QT syndrome. Which escalation best addresses the remaining trigger?',
 'Increase the underlying rate with temporary pacing or selected monitored isoproterenol',[
 ('Add a beta blocker to slow the sinus rate further','Beta blockade is important in congenital long-QT and adrenergically mediated syndromes. Further rate slowing can worsen acquired pause-dependent torsades when bradycardia remains the immediate trigger.'),
 ('Load sotalol to suppress the recurrent ventricular episodes','Sotalol has selected rhythm-control indications but prolongs repolarization and slows rate. Those effects can aggravate the acquired long-QT and bradycardic substrate described.'),
 ('Repeat shocks as the sole recurrence-prevention strategy','Defibrillation rescues sustained polymorphic VT, but does not remove the pause-dependent trigger between episodes. Continued electrical readiness must accompany treatment of the underlying mechanism.')],
 [S('Remove the Pauses That Continue to Initiate Arrhythmia','**ESC 2022 supports rate acceleration with isoproterenol or transvenous pacing for recurrent acquired long-QT torsades despite correction of triggers and magnesium.** Increasing rate reduces long pauses and can shorten repolarization.',
    'Confirm the acquired phenotype and the actual onset sequence. Review ongoing bradycardic drugs, AV block, persistent electrolyte abnormalities and delayed clearance of the culprit rather than assuming that discontinuation immediately removes its effect.',
    'Choose pacing or pharmacological acceleration according to the rhythm, vascular access, hemodynamics and available expertise. Titrate to suppression of pause-related recurrence with continuous ECG and pressure monitoring rather than applying a universal rate to every patient.'),
  S('Respect the Mechanism-Specific Exceptions','**Isoproterenol is not a general treatment for every polymorphic ventricular rhythm.** Adrenergic stimulation can be harmful in congenital long-QT disease, CPVT or active ischemia; the supplied exclusions matter.',
    'Temporary pacing can be particularly useful when clinically significant conduction disease sustains the bradycardia. Verify reliable capture and maintain external rescue capability while a temporary system is established.',
    'Sustained polymorphic VT still requires an immediate unsynchronized shock, and pulseless deterioration requires CPR and the shockable-arrest pathway. Rate acceleration prevents recurrence rather than replacing electrical rescue. Continue magnesium and electrolyte reassessment as clinically indicated, monitor QT recovery and account for renal function and residual drug exposure. Once the acquired problem resolves, reassess intrinsic rate and the need for ongoing pacing; temporary rescue does not by itself establish a permanent pacemaker indication. Persistent QT prolongation or a concerning personal or family history warrants investigation for inherited susceptibility rather than indefinite attribution to a transient drug exposure.')],['ESC2022','ALS2025'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Polymorphic VT Associated With Acute Myocardial Ischemia',
 'A patient has recurrent polymorphic VT during acute ischemic chest pain with dynamic ST elevation. Between episodes QTc is 420 ms and there is no pause-dependent onset. Sustained episodes have been defibrillated. Which next strategy best addresses recurrence?',
 'Urgently treat the coronary ischemia while using appropriate monitored antiarrhythmic support',[
 ('Treat as acquired torsades with magnesium as the sole definitive therapy','Magnesium is appropriate for long-QT torsades or deficiency. A normal interepisode QT with active ischemia requires treatment of the coronary driver rather than assuming magnesium alone resolves the cause.'),
 ('Use isoproterenol to increase myocardial rate and suppress presumed pauses','Isoproterenol can help selected acquired bradycardia-dependent torsades. Here there is no pause-dependent long-QT mechanism, and increasing myocardial demand can worsen active ischemia.'),
 ('Defer coronary treatment until antiarrhythmic loading has eliminated all ventricular ectopy','Antiarrhythmics can help stabilize recurrent arrhythmia, but they do not restore coronary flow. Waiting for complete electrical suppression can leave the ischemic driver untreated and prolong danger.')],
 [S('Normal-QT Polymorphic VT Is a Different Phenotype','**Acute myocardial ischemia is an important cause of polymorphic VT without prolonged QT.** Review symptoms, serial ECGs, hemodynamics and coronary evidence rather than assigning the torsades label from QRS variability alone.',
    'A normal QT does not prove ischemia in every patient; inherited syndromes, short-coupled triggers and other myocardial disorders can cause normal-QT polymorphic arrhythmia. The ischemic symptoms and dynamic ST elevation here supply the relevant additional evidence.',
    'Defibrillation treats sustained episodes immediately. Recurrent self-terminating runs still require continuous observation, pads and readiness because they can become sustained or deteriorate to VF.'),
  S('Treat the Trigger Alongside Electrical Stabilization','**AHA 2025 permits IV lidocaine or amiodarone and measures to treat myocardial ischemia for recurrences without prolonged QT.** Urgent coronary reperfusion when indicated addresses a driver that antiarrhythmics alone cannot remove.',
    'Selected beta blockade can reduce ischemic and adrenergic triggers when pressure, ventricular function and conduction permit. Avoid IV beta blockade in hypotension, acute decompensated HF, severe bradycardia or important AV block.',
    'Correct hypoxia and electrolyte abnormalities and review proarrhythmic drugs concurrently. Routine magnesium is not recommended solely for normal-QT polymorphic VT, although documented deficiency remains a separate treatment indication. Avoid importing pacing or isoproterenol from the acquired torsades pathway without evidence for that mechanism. After stabilization and coronary treatment, reassess ventricular function, infarct substrate and timing of the arrhythmia to plan secondary prevention; the acute ischemic setting does not supply an automatic universal ICD decision. Maintain careful distinction between an arrhythmia caused by a transient correctable trigger and persistent risk from established myocardial disease.')],['ALS2025','ESC2022'])

R['STORM2024']=('EHRA multisociety electrical storm consensus, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1093/europace/euae049')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Electrical Storm and Device-Confirmed Episodes',
 'An ICD patient has three distinct sustained monomorphic VT episodes within 12 hours, separated by 40 minutes. Stored electrograms confirm ventricular origin. Two episodes terminate with antitachycardia pacing and one with a shock. Which classification best fits the EHRA 2024 definition?',
 'Electrical storm, because three separated sustained ventricular episodes required intervention within 24 hours',[
 ('Clustered VT that falls short of storm because only one shock occurred','Appropriate ATP is an intervention that terminates a qualifying ventricular episode. Electrical storm is not restricted to three defibrillator shocks; counting shocks alone underestimates ATP-treated episodes.'),
 ('Incessant VT because multiple treatments occurred during the same day','Incessant VT describes persistent or near-continuously recurrent tachycardia rather than three distinct episodes separated by prolonged intervals. Device timing and episode boundaries matter.'),
 ('An unconfirmed shock cluster until three symptomatic episodes are documented','Symptoms and shock reports alone are imperfect, but stored electrograms here already establish three ventricular episodes. ATP-treated and asymptomatic episodes can satisfy the definition without three symptom reports.')],
 [S('Count Arrhythmic Episodes Rather Than Shocks','**EHRA 2024 defines electrical storm as at least three sustained ventricular-arrhythmia episodes within 24 hours, separated by at least five minutes, each requiring intervention for termination.** Appropriate ATP can qualify alongside cardioversion or defibrillation.',
    'Several shocks during one persistent episode do not automatically represent several separate ventricular episodes. Review electrograms, timing and whether the rhythm actually terminated between therapies.',
    'A patient reporting multiple shocks still requires verification because AF, oversensing or lead noise can cause inappropriate therapies. The treatment of inappropriate shocks differs from treatment of genuine recurrent VT.'),
  S('Interrogation Is Part of Immediate Clinical Assessment','**Assess circulation and treat active unstable arrhythmia while arranging prompt device interrogation.** The device record clarifies rhythm mechanism, cycle length, onset, ATP response and shock effectiveness.',
    'Check ECG, electrolytes, medications, renal function, ischemia and HF deterioration. A recognizable chronic scar substrate does not exclude an acute trigger superimposed on it.',
    'The definition identifies a high-risk emergency rather than a threshold below which recurrent ventricular arrhythmia is safe. Two serious episodes can still require urgent specialist care. Reduce distress and avoidable shocks with appropriate analgesia, sedation and individualized device programming while preserving rescue capability. Subsequent suppression can require antiarrhythmics, sympathetic blockade and ablation depending on substrate and response. Do not merely clear the device counter and discharge after a successful shock: the clustered event burden indicates electrical instability and possible hemodynamic deterioration. Document the number of verified episodes and interventions separately so later treatment decisions are based on the arrhythmias rather than an ambiguous shock count.')],['STORM2024','ESC2022'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Initial Stabilization and Trigger Search in Electrical Storm',
 'A patient with ischemic cardiomyopathy has recurrent ICD-treated VT. The patient is currently perfusing but distressed after repeated shocks, with increasing congestion and recent diuretic escalation. Which initial plan is most appropriate?',
 'Provide monitored stabilization, device review, analgesia or sedation, and a parallel search for reversible triggers',[
 ('Escalate antiarrhythmic medication before assessing electrolytes or HF','Antiarrhythmics may be necessary, but hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia and decompensated HF can perpetuate arrhythmia and increase drug toxicity. Trigger assessment should occur concurrently rather than after repeated empirical loading.'),
 ('Suspend ICD therapies before reviewing whether the shocks were appropriate','Temporary suspension can be appropriate for confirmed inappropriate therapies or selected monitored management. Empirical suppression before assessing genuine recurrent VT can remove needed protection; verify the rhythm and provide dependable external rescue whenever internal therapies are altered.'),
 ('Repeat shocks as needed and defer sedation until all ventricular episodes cease','Electrical rescue is essential for unstable episodes, but pain and anxiety increase sympathetic activation and can reinforce recurrence. Appropriate monitored analgesia or sedation is an active component of stabilization, not merely a later comfort measure.')],
 [S('Treat Circulatory Risk and Sympathetic Activation Together','**Electrical storm requires immediate assessment of perfusion, rhythm and the underlying cardiac substrate.** Treat an ongoing unstable ventricular arrhythmia promptly while organizing monitored specialist care.',
    'Repeated shocks cause pain, fear and adrenergic activation that can promote further arrhythmia. Use appropriate analgesia and sedation with blood-pressure and airway supervision; severe HF increases the risk of hemodynamic effects from sedative drugs.',
    'Interrogate the ICD to verify appropriate therapies and identify episodes that can be managed through better detection, ATP or other programming changes. Device reprogramming must preserve reliable rescue rather than simply eliminate audible or painful treatments.'),
  S('Search for Correctable Drivers Without Delaying Rescue','**Check potassium, magnesium, renal function, ischemia, drug exposure and HF status.** Diuretic changes can produce electrolyte depletion, while congestion and worsening output can increase electrical instability.',
    'Obtain the ECG and relevant laboratory and imaging assessment, and review missed medication, new QT-prolonging drugs, infection and other systemic stressors. Treat a demonstrated coronary or inflammatory problem through its appropriate pathway.',
    'Combine trigger correction with substrate-appropriate antiarrhythmic and sympathetic treatment rather than expecting one isolated intervention to solve every case. Refractory monomorphic storm can require urgent ablation evaluation, and circulatory deterioration may require advanced support. Alert the electrophysiology and critical-care teams early when recurrence is frequent. A temporarily regular pulse after a shock does not establish that the storm has ended or that discharge is safe. Continue reassessment of urine output, congestion, perfusion and drug adverse effects, because renal and hemodynamic changes can rapidly alter the safety of treatment originally selected.')],['STORM2024','ESC2022'])

R['PROPRANOLOL']=('Propranolol versus metoprolol in ICD electrical storm randomized trial, 2018','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2018.02.056')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Sympathetic Blockade in Structural-Heart-Disease Electrical Storm',
 'An ICD patient with structural heart disease has recurrent monomorphic VT despite trigger correction and IV amiodarone. BP is 122/74 mmHg without shock, acute decompensated HF, bronchospasm or significant AV block. Which additional pharmacological strategy is supported?',
 'Add carefully monitored beta blockade, considering a nonselective agent such as propranolol',[
 ('Avoid beta blockade because amiodarone already supplies complete sympathetic suppression','Amiodarone has antiadrenergic effects, but they do not make additional beta blockade redundant. Combination treatment is supported in appropriate structural-heart-disease electrical storm, with attention to tolerance.'),
 ('Add sotalol to IV amiodarone as the preferred way to provide beta blockade','Sotalol also prolongs repolarization, so combining it with amiodarone adds QT-related and hemodynamic hazards. A beta-blocking strategy should not automatically mean simultaneous class III loading.'),
 ('Give verapamil to suppress the presumed scar-related ventricular circuit','Verapamil is useful for specifically established sensitive fascicular VT and selected supraventricular rhythms. It is not the standard sympathetic-treatment adjunct for scar-related storm and can impair pressure and contractility.')],
 [S('Adrenergic Activation Helps Sustain the Storm','**ESC guidance recommends beta blockade, with nonselective agents preferred, alongside IV amiodarone for electrical storm in structural heart disease unless contraindicated.** Repeated shocks, pain and HF can amplify sympathetic drive.',
    'Propranolol blocks beta-1 and beta-2 receptors; metoprolol predominantly blocks beta-1 receptors. Agent selection must also account for bronchospasm, bradycardia, AV conduction and ventricular performance.',
    'A short-acting IV beta blocker can be useful in selected closely monitored situations where rapid titration and withdrawal are desirable. Short duration does not remove the possibility of profound hypotension or deterioration.'),
  S('Interpret the Comparative Trial and Respect Tolerance','**A randomized trial enrolled 60 ICD patients receiving IV amiodarone and compared oral propranolol 160 mg/day with metoprolol 200 mg/day.** It supported better arrhythmia suppression with propranolol in that selected setting.',
    'These are study regimens, not mandatory starting doses for every acutely ill patient. Assess pressure, perfusion, congestion and conduction before and during titration; the trial does not justify nonselective blockade during active bronchospasm or uncontrolled shock.',
    'Combine medication with analgesia or sedation, appropriate device programming and continued correction of triggers. Recurrent scar-related VT despite treatment warrants early ablation assessment rather than indefinite addition of drugs. Monitor for additive bradycardia and myocardial suppression with amiodarone. If the patient deteriorates, reassess whether the rhythm, underlying pump failure or treatment is responsible and arrange advanced support when appropriate. Sympathetic blockade reduces a promoting influence but does not remove infarct scar or replace an independently indicated ICD. The supplied preserved pressure and absence of major contraindications support carefully monitored escalation rather than blanket avoidance or indiscriminate multidrug loading.')],['ESC2022','STORM2024','PROPRANOLOL'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Escalation for Drug-Refractory Electrical Storm',
 'A patient has recurrent appropriate ICD shocks for monomorphic VT despite trigger correction, tolerated antiarrhythmics and sympathetic treatment. Anxiety and recurrent episodes persist, with worsening perfusion. Which escalation best matches contemporary consensus?',
 'Arrange expert-center care with deep sedation when appropriate, urgent ablation assessment and individualized autonomic or circulatory support',[
 ('Continue repeated drug loading on the ward until each available antiarrhythmic has been tried','Further drugs may have selected roles, but repeated empirical loading can add hypotension and proarrhythmia. Refractory storm with worsening perfusion requires escalation of setting and a mechanism-directed plan.'),
 ('Perform stellate ganglion block and defer ablation assessment until a later recurrence','Stellate blockade can reduce sympathetic drive and serve as a bridge in selected refractory cases. A transient response does not establish durable substrate control; refractory monomorphic storm warrants concurrent ablation assessment rather than an obligatory wait for another episode.'),
 ('Start mechanical circulatory support and defer arrhythmia-directed intervention until ventricular recovery','Support can maintain perfusion during stabilization or ablation, but it does not remove the arrhythmogenic substrate. Ongoing VT can itself prevent recovery, so support should have a defined bridge objective with parallel rhythm treatment.')],
 [S('Escalate the Setting Before Progressive Collapse','**Drug-refractory electrical storm requires coordinated electrophysiology, intensive-care and anesthesia expertise.** Repeated shocks and worsening perfusion can create a cycle of adrenergic activation, myocardial dysfunction and further arrhythmia.',
    'Deep sedation with mechanical ventilation may be appropriate to suppress sympathetic activation and permit controlled treatment. Prepare for sedative-related hypotension and negative inotropy rather than assuming that a quieter patient is necessarily hemodynamically safer.',
    'Urgent catheter-ablation evaluation is important for refractory monomorphic VT. Stabilization and procedural planning should proceed together, with transfer to an experienced center when local capability is insufficient.'),
  S('Use Bridging Therapies With Defined Objectives','**Autonomic modulation, including selected stellate ganglion blockade, can reduce arrhythmia burden when conventional treatment is inadequate.** Much of the supporting evidence is observational, so avoid presenting a temporary response as proof of durable cure.',
    'Review anticoagulation, bleeding, airway and local procedural risks before regional interventions. Bilateral blockade has additional respiratory implications and requires appropriate expertise and support.',
    'Mechanical circulatory support may be considered for refractory storm with cardiogenic shock or selected high-risk procedures. Choose the device and timing according to ventricular failure, vascular access, oxygenation needs and the intended bridge, accounting for bleeding, limb ischemia and other complications. No support device independently treats every arrhythmia mechanism. Continue device interrogation, trigger correction and drug-toxicity surveillance during escalation. A successful block or period without shocks should prompt reassessment of the durable plan rather than cancellation of substrate evaluation. Distinguish an acute rescue bridge from later long-term HF, ablation and sudden-death-prevention decisions, each of which remains necessary according to the underlying disease.')],['STORM2024','ESC2022'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Ablation After Recurrent VT Despite Amiodarone',
 'A patient with prior MI, ischemic cardiomyopathy and an ICD has recurrent symptomatic sustained monomorphic VT with appropriate shocks despite chronic amiodarone. Adherence, electrolytes, HF status and acute ischemia have been assessed and corrected where necessary. Which next strategy is preferred under ESC 2022 guidance?',
 'Refer for VT catheter ablation rather than default escalation of antiarrhythmic drugs',[
 ('Increase amiodarone as the preferred strategy before considering ablation','Higher dosing can be considered in selected individual circumstances, but recurrent symptomatic VT or shocks despite chronic amiodarone is a guideline setting favoring ablation over drug escalation.'),
 ('Add mexiletine as the preferred strategy before considering ablation','Mexiletine can be an adjunct when ablation is unavailable, unsuccessful or unsuitable. It is not the preferred default over ablation for the eligible recurrent postinfarction presentation described.'),
 ('Increase ICD shock energy as the sole next intervention','Shock programming and defibrillation efficacy require review, but these shocks have appropriately terminated VT. A stronger shock does not prevent the scar circuit from recurring or replace substrate treatment.')],
 [S('Treat Recurrent Scar Arrhythmia Beyond Episode Termination','**ESC 2022 recommends catheter ablation in preference to antiarrhythmic escalation for recurrent symptomatic sustained monomorphic VT or ICD shocks despite chronic amiodarone in coronary disease.** The aim is to reduce recurrent arrhythmia and device-therapy burden.',
    'Confirm appropriate ventricular therapies through interrogation and reassess acute triggers. A history of scar-related VT does not exclude a new coronary event, electrolyte disturbance, drug interaction or HF deterioration.',
    'Ablation evaluation considers ventricular function, scar location, vascular access, thrombus, comorbidity and the likelihood of tolerating mapping. An experienced center may need substrate mapping or circulatory support for unstable inducible rhythms.'),
  S('Keep the Benefits and Limits Clear','**Ablation can reduce recurrence without eliminating the underlying cardiomyopathy or every future ventricular arrhythmia.** ICD protection, HF treatment and appropriate device programming remain separate components of care.',
    'The VANISH randomized comparison supports ablation over drug escalation in ischemic cardiomyopathy with an ICD and VT despite antiarrhythmic therapy. Its composite outcome should not be relabelled as a proven isolated mortality benefit.',
    'Explain vascular, perforation, stroke and hemodynamic risks and the possibility of repeat procedures. Drug treatment can still be needed after ablation, particularly when residual substrate or another arrhythmia remains. If ablation is unsuitable or unavailable, individualized medication and autonomic strategies may provide control, but their use does not erase the rationale for specialist referral. Do not make a patient endure repeated shocks merely to complete an arbitrary sequence of additional drugs when the guideline-preferred next strategy is already applicable. Acute instability still requires immediate rescue while the durable treatment is organized.')],['ESC2022','STORM2024'])

R['VANISH']=('VANISH randomized trial, 2016','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1513614')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'The Original VANISH Trial: Comparator and Outcome',
 'Which statement correctly describes the original VANISH trial in ischemic cardiomyopathy with an ICD and VT despite antiarrhythmic therapy?',
 'Ablation reduced a composite of death, VT storm or appropriate ICD shock compared with escalation of antiarrhythmic therapy',[
 ('Ablation was compared with ICD implantation in patients without a device','Participants already had an ICD. The trial addressed recurrent VT treatment on that background, not replacement of device implantation with ablation.'),
 ('Ablation was compared with no antiarrhythmic treatment in previously untreated VT','The defining problem was VT despite antiarrhythmic therapy. The control strategy escalated medication rather than withholding treatment or studying a drug-naive first episode.'),
 ('Ablation established a significant isolated survival advantage as its primary endpoint','The primary endpoint combined death, VT storm and appropriate shock. A significant composite result does not establish a significant mortality reduction considered alone.')],
 [S('The Comparison Was Against Medication Escalation','**VANISH enrolled patients with ischemic cardiomyopathy, an ICD and VT despite antiarrhythmic drugs.** Patients were randomized to catheter ablation with continued baseline drug treatment or a prespecified escalation strategy.',
    'Escalation depended on prior therapy: initiation or increased dosing of amiodarone, or addition of mexiletine when already receiving a substantial amiodarone regimen. The trial therefore did not compare ablation with an untreated control.',
    'The setting differs from initial ablation versus initial drug treatment, which is the question addressed by VANISH2. Keep the trial names, entry criteria and comparators distinct.'),
  S('Interpret a Composite Benefit Accurately','**The primary outcome occurred in 59.1% with ablation and 68.5% with escalated therapy over a mean follow-up of approximately 28 months.** The hazard ratio was 0.72, with a 95% confidence interval of 0.53–0.98.',
    'The endpoint combined death, VT storm or an appropriate ICD shock. The result supports reducing clinically important events, but it does not prove that each component improved independently or that ablation made an ICD unnecessary.',
    'Both approaches have harms: ablation carries procedural risk, while drug escalation can increase pulmonary, hepatic, neurological and hemodynamic toxicity. Interpret baseline-drug subgroup findings as additional context rather than replacing the randomized overall comparison with an unrestricted rule. Selection still requires assessment of scar, ventricular function, comorbidity and local expertise. Continue HF and coronary treatment, appropriate ICD programming and surveillance after the selected intervention. A patient can benefit from fewer storms and shocks while retaining substantial mortality risk from pump failure or other disease, so distinguish arrhythmia control from a promise of cure or survival extension.')],['VANISH','ESC2022'],style='direct')

R['VANISH2']=('VANISH2 randomized trial, online 2024; print 2025','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2409501')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Initial VT Ablation: Interpreting VANISH2',
 'A patient with prior MI and an ICD develops clinically significant VT and is discussing initial ablation versus an antiarrhythmic strategy. Which interpretation best describes VANISH2?',
 'Initial ablation reduced the prespecified composite outcome compared with a protocol-selected sotalol or amiodarone strategy in the studied ischemic population',[
 ('The trial required failure of chronic amiodarone before randomization','That description resembles the prior-drug-failure question addressed by the original VANISH trial. VANISH2 evaluated an initial ablation strategy rather than requiring chronic amiodarone failure.'),
 ('The trial established ablation as a substitute for ICD protection','Participants had ICD protection, and device-related outcomes formed part of the assessment. The comparison did not test removing or withholding the ICD after successful ablation.'),
 ('The trial compared initial ablation with mexiletine added to high-dose amiodarone in every control patient','The drug strategy used sotalol or amiodarone according to prespecified eligibility. A uniform mexiletine add-on comparator misidentifies the treatment tested.')],
 [S('Distinguish Initial Strategy From Rescue After Drug Failure','**VANISH2 studied patients with previous MI, an ICD and clinically significant VT.** Qualifying events included storm, appropriate ICD therapy or sustained VT requiring emergency treatment.',
    'Patients were randomized to ablation or an antiarrhythmic strategy using sotalol or amiodarone according to protocol criteria. Ablation was scheduled within fourteen days after randomization.',
    'The trial broadens the discussion beyond waiting for repeated drug failure, but it does not make every incidental NSVT run or every nonischemic substrate equivalent to its entry population.'),
  S('Interpret the Outcome and the Remaining Risks','**At median follow-up of 4.3 years, the primary outcome occurred in 50.7% with ablation versus 60.6% with drug therapy.** The hazard ratio was 0.75, with a 95% confidence interval of 0.58–0.97.',
    'The endpoint counted all-cause death throughout follow-up and specified arrhythmia outcomes more than fourteen days after randomization: storm, appropriate ICD shock or treated sustained VT below the ICD detection rate. It was not an isolated survival endpoint or a guarantee of freedom from all VT.',
    'Discuss procedural adverse events alongside chronic drug toxicity and individual suitability. Scar distribution, ventricular function, frailty, comorbidity and center experience affect the choice. The finding should inform an early electrophysiology discussion, not be converted into an assertion that drugs are inappropriate in every patient. ICD follow-up and HF and coronary treatment remain essential because ablation does not remove all sudden-death or pump-failure risk. Maintain the distinction from original VANISH: both support an ablation role, but one addressed escalation after antiarrhythmic failure and the other an initial strategy in a selected ischemic population. Verify the actual qualifying event before applying either result to a substantially different rhythm history.')],['VANISH2','ESC2022'],style='direct')

R['VAABLATION2019']=('HRS/EHRA/APHRS/LAHRS ventricular arrhythmia ablation consensus, 2019','https://doi.org/10.1002/joa3.12185')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Scar Reentry and Substrate Mapping',
 'Years after MI, a patient has recurrent sustained monomorphic VT. Induced clinical VT causes hypotension too rapidly for prolonged activation mapping. Which approach best explains a useful ablation strategy?',
 'Map abnormal scar channels and electrograms during a tolerated rhythm to target the reentry substrate',[
 ('Restrict mapping to prolonged activation and entrainment during the hypotensive clinical VT','Activation and entrainment are useful when VT can be sustained safely. Repeated prolonged induction in this poorly tolerated rhythm can worsen perfusion; substrate mapping provides information during a more tolerable rhythm.'),
 ('Target the earliest isolated PVC as the presumed source of the sustained scar VT','Activation mapping of a trigger PVC is valuable for selected focal arrhythmias or PVC-triggered VF. In established scar reentry, an incidental PVC need not identify the critical sustaining channel and cannot substitute for circuit or substrate assessment.'),
 ('Use a single pace-map match as proof of the entire critical reentry isthmus','Pace mapping can identify an exit region or support localization. A matching surface QRS does not by itself prove participation of that site in the essential channel or define the full circuit.')],
 [S('Surviving Myocardium Within Fibrosis Supports Reentry','**Postinfarction monomorphic VT commonly uses slow conduction through surviving myocardial bundles within or along scar.** Fixed and functional block permit a reentrant circuit rather than a simple uniformly dead region.',
    'Different exits from related channels can produce different VT morphologies. A stable QRS pattern during one episode does not prove that only one possible circuit exists in the ventricle.',
    'The substrate can be endocardial, intramural or epicardial. Nonischemic cardiomyopathy can have a different distribution, so surface or endocardial findings do not map every disease identically.'),
  S('Choose Mapping According to Hemodynamic Tolerance','**Activation and entrainment mapping can define circuit participation when VT is sufficiently tolerated.** When sustained mapping is unsafe, substrate assessment during sinus or paced rhythm can identify abnormal potentials and conduction channels.',
    'Low voltage, delayed or fractionated signals and local abnormal ventricular activities help characterize potential targets. Integrate imaging, electroanatomic mapping and any available tachycardia evidence rather than treating every low-voltage point as an essential isthmus.',
    'Pace mapping approximates the ventricular exit pattern and can complement other methods, but its spatial and mechanistic limitations prevent equating one excellent match with proof of the entire circuit. Ablation may target channels or a broader abnormal substrate according to the case; procedural endpoints include clinical VT noninducibility and appropriate substrate assessment. Acute noninducibility does not guarantee lifelong freedom from recurrence because additional circuits, inaccessible tissue or evolving disease can remain. Plan for hemodynamic deterioration and selected support when needed, and continue independent HF and ICD management. The inability to sustain VT for detailed activation mapping does not by itself make ablation impossible; it changes the mapping and procedural-risk strategy.')],['VAABLATION2019','ESC2022'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Suspected PVC-Induced Cardiomyopathy',
 'A patient has new global LV dysfunction with LVEF 38% and a predominantly monomorphic PVC burden of 24% on representative ambulatory monitoring. Coronary assessment and initial laboratory evaluation are unrevealing. Which interpretation is most appropriate?',
 'PVC-induced or PVC-aggravated cardiomyopathy is plausible and warrants substrate assessment and targeted suppression',[
 ('The PVCs are established as a consequence of cardiomyopathy and cannot contribute to dysfunction','Myocardial disease can generate ectopy, but frequent PVCs can also cause or aggravate LV dysfunction. The relationship requires investigation rather than assuming a single direction of causation.'),
 ('The burden proves PVC-induced cardiomyopathy without further etiological evaluation','A high burden raises suspicion but does not exclude occult scar, inflammation or another cardiomyopathy. Response to successful suppression can help establish the causal contribution.'),
 ('The burden is below the range associated with PVC-related dysfunction','ESC discusses concern from approximately ten-percent burden, with increasing risk above twenty percent. Twenty-four percent is clinically relevant, although a numerical threshold alone does not establish causation.')],
 [S('Burden Raises Suspicion Rather Than Proving Causality','**Consider PVC-induced cardiomyopathy in unexplained reduced EF with frequent ventricular ectopy.** ESC 2022 identifies a burden of approximately 10% as an important concern threshold, with risk increasing further above 20%.',
    'Burden is the proportion of ventricular complexes represented by PVCs, not simply a pulse count. Day-to-day variation can affect a short recording, so confirm that monitoring reasonably represents the patient’s usual rhythm.',
    'Predominantly monomorphic ectopy can suggest a targetable source, but multiple factors influence risk and recovery. A high burden does not mean that every patient develops cardiomyopathy.'),
  S('Investigate the Substrate and Demonstrate the Response','**Assess history, ECG, echocardiography and appropriate coronary and myocardial imaging.** CMR can identify scar or another disease that makes PVC-aggravated pre-existing cardiomyopathy more likely than a purely ectopy-induced process.',
    'When burden is below ten percent and dysfunction is otherwise unexplained, look particularly carefully for alternative causes and consider whether recording variability underestimates ectopy. The threshold is a clinical guide, not a biological certainty.',
    'Treat HF while planning appropriate suppression, often with ablation when PVC-induced cardiomyopathy is suspected and the source is suitable. Reassess PVC burden and ventricular function after effective treatment; improvement supports a causal contribution. Recovery is not necessarily immediate or complete if another substrate coexists. A single improved ECG does not demonstrate durable burden reduction. Patients with frequent PVCs but preserved function need periodic ventricular-function reassessment rather than automatic labeling as cardiomyopathy or universal invasive treatment. Distinguish an indication to investigate and treat a potentially reversible driver from a claim that the recorded percentage independently dictates a permanent ICD.')],['ESC2022','VAABLATION2019'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Ablation for PVC-Induced Ventricular Dysfunction',
 'A patient has persistent predominantly monomorphic PVCs comprising 22% of beats, LVEF 35% and symptoms despite a tolerated beta blocker. CMR shows no alternative explanatory substrate. The focus is considered technically suitable for ablation. Which strategy best addresses the suspected reversible driver?',
 'Offer PVC catheter ablation with concurrent HF care and subsequent burden and EF reassessment',[
 ('Defer PVC treatment until HF medication alone normalizes EF','HF medication remains necessary, but persistent ectopy may prevent recovery when it is a major driver. Waiting for EF to normalize before treating that driver can delay a useful intervention.'),
 ('Implant a primary-prevention ICD immediately instead of addressing the PVC burden','Device protection can later be indicated under the relevant disease and timing criteria. It does not suppress the reversible ectopic driver, and newly recognized potentially reversible dysfunction requires treatment and reassessment.'),
 ('Choose long-term amiodarone before discussing ablation because its suppression is noninvasive','Amiodarone can suppress ectopy in selected circumstances, especially if other strategies are unsuitable. Its organ toxicity and the established ablation role make it an inappropriate mandatory step before discussing an eligible focus.')],
 [S('Target a Potentially Reversible Cause','**Catheter ablation is a first-line treatment for PVC-induced cardiomyopathy under ESC 2022 guidance.** The selected predominantly monomorphic source and absence of a better explanation support a targeted approach.',
    'A prior failure of several antiarrhythmics is not required for every eligible patient. Discuss alternatives and patient preference, but do not make a fixed sequence of toxic drugs a prerequisite for treating the suspected driver.',
    'Continue appropriate HF therapy and manage congestion. Eliminating ectopy does not immediately replace support for a ventricle that is currently dysfunctional.'),
  S('Define Success Beyond Acute Disappearance','**Assess durable PVC reduction and subsequent ventricular recovery.** Repeat ambulatory recording and imaging are more informative than a brief postprocedure tracing without ectopy.',
    'Ablation success and risk depend on origin, accessibility, number of morphologies and any myocardial disease. Proximity to the conduction system, coronary arteries or other structures changes the procedural discussion.',
    'Explain vascular injury, tamponade and location-specific complications, recurrence and the possible need for repeat treatment. Persistent dysfunction after effective suppression prompts reassessment for another cardiomyopathy and for device indications after the appropriate treatment interval. Do not promise complete recovery or equate a technically successful focal lesion with elimination of every future ventricular-arrhythmia risk. Conversely, an asymptomatic person with preserved function and frequent PVCs is a different decision: periodic assessment is important, and ablation for repeatedly very high burden may be considered selectively, but prognosis benefit is not established as a universal indication. Here ventricular dysfunction and a plausible dominant source provide a stronger treatment rationale than the burden percentage alone.')],['ESC2022','VAABLATION2019'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Outflow-Tract Ventricular Tachycardia',
 'A young adult has catecholamine-associated monomorphic VT with an LBBB-like pattern and inferior axis. Baseline ECG, echo and directed structural evaluation are reassuring. Adenosine terminates a monitored episode. Which interpretation is most likely?',
 'Idiopathic outflow-tract VT, commonly related to cAMP-mediated triggered activity',[
 ('Typical slow–fast AVNRT established by adenosine termination','AVNRT often terminates with adenosine, but some ventricular tachycardias do as well. The demonstrated ventricular phenotype and outflow-type morphology require interpretation rather than assigning a nodal mechanism from the drug response alone.'),
 ('Left posterior fascicular reentrant VT','The common posterior fascicular phenotype has an RBBB-like pattern and superior or leftward axis and is typically verapamil sensitive. The supplied LBBB-like inferior-axis pattern and adrenergic association favor an outflow source.'),
 ('Scar-mediated macroreentrant VT established by the broad QRS','Scar reentry is important when structural disease is present. QRS width alone does not establish scar, and this selected reassuring evaluation and adenosine-sensitive outflow phenotype favor triggered idiopathic VT.')],
 [S('Use Morphology as a Localization Clue','**RVOT arrhythmias commonly have an LBBB-like QRS and inferior axis.** Activation spreads away from a superior right ventricular source toward the inferior leads.',
    'LV outflow and adjacent structures can produce overlapping appearances. Precordial transition and more detailed mapping help refine origin, but a single broad morphology should not be treated as an exact anatomical map.',
    'An apparently idiopathic outflow rhythm still requires an appropriate structural and family assessment. ARVC and other substrates can mimic a right ventricular origin, especially when syncope, abnormal sinus ECG or multiple morphologies are present.'),
  S('Connect Triggered Activity to the Drug Response','**Many idiopathic outflow VTs involve cAMP-dependent calcium loading and delayed afterdepolarization-mediated triggered activity.** Exercise or emotional stress can increase frequency, and adenosine can terminate selected episodes.',
    'Adenosine termination therefore does not uniquely establish AVNRT or AVRT. In an uncertain wide-complex rhythm, its consideration is restricted to an appropriate stable, regular monomorphic phenotype with rescue readiness.',
    'Treatment depends on symptoms, burden, ventricular function and preference. Beta blockers can be useful for an adrenergic pattern, while catheter ablation has an established role for symptomatic idiopathic RVOT VT. Unstable sustained episodes still require electrical treatment regardless of a previously reassuring idiopathic label. Document the clinical ECG before definitive treatment, and reassess if behavior changes or ventricular dysfunction appears. A benign usual course does not justify ignoring new syncope or assuming every later wide rhythm has the same mechanism. Mechanism, localization and exclusion of relevant disease are complementary assessments rather than interchangeable conclusions from one response.')],['ESC2022','VAABLATION2019'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'First-Line Ablation for Symptomatic Idiopathic RVOT VT',
 'A patient has recurrent symptomatic idiopathic RVOT VT, preserved ventricular function and a reassuring structural evaluation. The patient prefers definitive treatment and is an appropriate procedural candidate. Under ESC 2022 guidance, which strategy can be offered without requiring failure of several antiarrhythmics?',
 'Catheter ablation after discussion of procedural benefits, risks and alternatives',[
 ('Chronic amiodarone as the required first treatment before ablation','Amiodarone has selected roles when other strategies fail or cannot be used, but its systemic toxicity makes it an unsuitable mandatory first step for an otherwise suitable idiopathic RVOT focus.'),
 ('Trials of both sotalol and flecainide before ablation is considered','Drug treatment is an option in selected patients, but the named ESC framework does not require these sequential failures before first-line ablation for symptomatic idiopathic RVOT VT.'),
 ('ICD implantation as the preferred definitive treatment of the focus','An ICD terminates dangerous ventricular arrhythmias but does not eliminate the focus. An adequately evaluated idiopathic RVOT presentation without another sudden-death indication does not automatically call for implantation.')],
 [S('Apply the Recommendation to the Named Origin','**ESC 2022 recommends catheter ablation as first-line treatment for symptomatic idiopathic RVOT or left fascicular VT/PVCs.** The recommendation is origin-specific and should not be generalized to every ventricular focus or every patient with ectopy.',
    'Confirm that symptoms relate to the recorded rhythm and that the evaluation adequately supports an idiopathic substrate. New syncope, unusual morphology or structural findings can require additional investigation before proceeding.',
    'Discuss observation or medication when symptoms are minor, patient preference differs or the procedural balance is unfavorable. A first-line recommendation does not remove shared decision-making.'),
  S('Compare Alternatives and Verify the Outcome','**Beta blockers, suitable nondihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers or selected flecainide can be considered when ablation is unavailable, unwanted or particularly risky.** Drug choice still depends on EF, conduction, ischemic disease and other contraindications.',
    'Ablation maps the clinical source and aims for durable elimination of the targeted arrhythmia. Risks include vascular injury and perforation, with additional anatomical concerns depending on the actual mapped site.',
    'Apparent RVOT morphology can reflect an adjacent origin, so mapping may change the approach and risk discussion. Follow symptoms and rhythm burden after treatment; a brief quiet recording is not proof of lifelong success. Recurrence can require repeat recording and assessment rather than automatic attribution to failure of the same focus. Continue ventricular-function surveillance when burden has been high, and investigate any new disease features. Unlike scar-related VT or inherited high-risk syndromes, successful focus treatment here is not automatically accompanied by a separate ICD indication. Distinguish symptom relief and burden reduction from claims of a proven survival benefit in every structurally normal patient.')],['ESC2022','VAABLATION2019'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Verapamil-Sensitive Fascicular Ventricular Tachycardia',
 'A young adult has recurrent tolerated VT with an RBBB-like QRS and superior axis. Structural evaluation is reassuring, and a previous EPS established posterior fascicular reentry. Which acute drug can specifically terminate this mechanism when pressure and conduction permit?',
 'IV verapamil under continuous monitoring',[
 ('IV adenosine','Adenosine treats AV-node-dependent SVT and can terminate selected triggered outflow VT. Reentrant posterior fascicular VT is characteristically verapamil sensitive rather than defined by adenosine sensitivity.'),
 ('IV diltiazem','Diltiazem is useful for selected supraventricular rate-control problems, but membership in the nondihydropyridine calcium-channel-blocker group does not establish equivalent efficacy for fascicular reentry. Verapamil is the established mechanism-directed agent here.'),
 ('IV amiodarone','Amiodarone is a general option for selected tolerated ventricular tachycardias and may have antiarrhythmic effects here. The question asks for the specific established sensitivity of this confirmed fascicular circuit, for which verapamil is preferred.')],
 [S('Recognize the Established Purkinje-Related Mechanism','**Idiopathic posterior fascicular reentrant VT commonly produces an RBBB-like QRS with a superior axis.** The QRS can be relatively narrow compared with other VTs because specialized ventricular conduction tissue participates.',
    'The slow, verapamil-sensitive component is part of a Purkinje-related reentry circuit. A response does not imply that the tachycardia originated in the AV node.',
    'Anterior fascicular VT more often has an RBBB-like pattern with inferior axis, while upper septal variants can be narrower with a normal or rightward axis. These patterns guide assessment but do not replace electrophysiological confirmation when the diagnosis is uncertain.'),
  S('Keep This Exception Separate From Undifferentiated WCT','**IV verapamil is appropriate for an established tolerated sensitive fascicular VT, with ECG and blood-pressure monitoring.** It is not an empirical diagnostic trial for an undifferentiated wide-complex rhythm.',
    'Review pressure, ventricular function, conduction disease and interacting nodal drugs before administration. If unstable, prioritize electrical treatment rather than waiting to demonstrate pharmacological sensitivity.',
    'For recurrent symptomatic idiopathic fascicular VT, ESC supports catheter ablation as a first-line definitive option after discussion of risks and alternatives. Mapping aims to interrupt the relevant circuit while limiting conduction-system injury. Do not assume that every RBBB/superior-axis VT is idiopathic: scar-related ventricular circuits and other diagnoses can overlap in appearance. Prior structural and EPS evidence in this stem makes the specific drug choice defensible. Follow symptoms and document recurrence after treatment, and reassess any new disease features. The key prescribing boundary is a confirmed mechanism with adequate hemodynamic margin, not the misconception that verapamil is safe whenever a patient with wide-complex tachycardia remains awake.')],['ESC2022','VAABLATION2019'])

R['CMP2023']=('ESC cardiomyopathy guideline, 2023','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehad194')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Right Ventricular Arrhythmia With Cardiomyopathy Red Flags',
 'A young adult has ventricular tachycardia with an LBBB-like morphology. There is unexplained exertional syncope, right-precordial T-wave inversion on the sinus ECG and a first-degree relative with premature sudden death. A screening echo is reported as normal. Which next approach is most appropriate?',
 'Investigate arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy with expert imaging and integrated electrical, family and genetic assessment',[
 ('Classify the rhythm as idiopathic RVOT VT from the LBBB-like morphology alone','LBBB-like ventricular activation suggests a right ventricular origin but does not establish a benign idiopathic substrate. Syncope, sinus-ECG abnormalities and family history raise concern for cardiomyopathy.'),
 ('Exclude arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy because screening echocardiography is normal','Early or localized disease can be missed on an initial echo. CMR and a broader phenotype assessment are appropriate when clinical and electrical red flags persist.'),
 ('Diagnose ARVC solely from right-precordial T-wave inversion','Repolarization abnormalities contribute to the assessment, but age, conduction pattern and alternative causes matter. Diagnosis requires an integrated framework rather than a single ECG sign.')],
 [S('A Ventricular Origin Is Not an Etiological Diagnosis','**Right ventricular tachycardia can occur in idiopathic outflow arrhythmia or arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy.** Similar tachycardia morphology does not make their prognosis or family implications equivalent.',
    'Exertional or unexplained syncope, premature familial sudden death, abnormal sinus ECG and multiple ventricular morphologies increase concern for a disease substrate. Absence of one classic sign does not exclude it.',
    'A normal initial echo can coexist with early or regional disease. Expert assessment should review the images and determine whether a dedicated CMR and additional evaluation are needed.'),
  S('Combine Imaging With Electrical and Familial Evidence','**ARVC assessment integrates structural and functional abnormalities, tissue findings, ECG features, ventricular arrhythmias and family or genetic evidence.** Apply a named diagnostic framework rather than accumulating nonspecific findings without context.',
    'CMR evaluates RV volumes, global and regional function and tissue characteristics, and can identify LV involvement. Interpret apparent regional abnormalities cautiously to avoid mistaking normal variants or athletic adaptation for disease.',
    'Review family history and consider genetic counseling and phenotype-directed testing. A pathogenic variant can guide family evaluation, while a variant of uncertain significance is not a standalone diagnosis. If disease is established, address exercise advice, disease-specific arrhythmic risk and relative screening as well as symptom control. Ablation can reduce VT burden but does not cure a progressive inherited substrate or automatically remove an ICD indication. This differs from a genuinely reassuring isolated RVOT focus. Continue follow-up when suspicion remains but diagnostic criteria are not yet met, because phenotype can evolve and new family information can change the assessment.')],['CMP2023','ESC2022'])

R['CAST']=('CAST encainide/flecainide mortality report, 1991','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM199103213241201')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Ventricular Ectopy Suppression After Myocardial Infarction',
 'A patient with prior MI, ventricular scar and LVEF 36% has mildly symptomatic PVCs. Flecainide markedly reduces the Holter PVC count during a proposed treatment trial. Which interpretation should guide continued prescribing?',
 'Do not infer clinical benefit from PVC suppression; class IC treatment is unsafe in this infarct-scar setting',[
 ('Continue flecainide because documented suppression establishes reduced sudden-death risk','Suppression is a surrogate electrophysiological effect. CAST demonstrated excess mortality despite successful ectopy suppression in the relevant post-MI population.'),
 ('Continue flecainide if a beta blocker is added to prevent proarrhythmia','AV nodal protection can reduce rapid flutter conduction in selected AF patients receiving class IC drugs. It does not neutralize the ventricular proarrhythmic risk of flecainide in infarct scar.'),
 ('Substitute propafenone because the concern applies specifically to the two CAST drugs','Drug-specific trial evidence informs a broader class IC restriction in prior MI or significant scar. Switching to another potent class IC sodium-channel blocker does not establish safety in this substrate.')],
 [S('A Successful Surrogate Can Accompany Clinical Harm','**CAST tested whether suppression of post-MI ventricular ectopy improved outcomes.** Encainide and flecainide increased mortality despite their ability to suppress asymptomatic or mildly symptomatic ventricular arrhythmias.',
    'The finding separates reduction in an observed PVC count from prevention of death. Selecting only patients whose ectopy responded did not make active therapy safe.',
    'Potent sodium-channel blockade can promote dangerous conduction disturbances in diseased myocardium. A reassuring short recording or lack of early symptoms does not exclude later ventricular proarrhythmia.'),
  S('Choose Treatment According to Substrate and Clinical Objective','**Avoid flecainide and related class IC strategies in the supplied prior-MI/scar presentation.** Their use for selected idiopathic ventricular arrhythmias or AF without relevant structural disease is a different setting.',
    'Optimize coronary and HF care, assess reversible contributors and quantify the ectopic burden and its relationship to symptoms or ventricular dysfunction. Consider an ablation strategy when a clinically consequential source is suitable.',
    'Evaluate ICD eligibility through the appropriate EF, symptom, timing and survival criteria rather than using the apparent response to a suppressive drug. Other antiarrhythmics also have specific indications and harms; CAST does not mean every ventricular arrhythmia should be left untreated. It means that treatment must demonstrate an acceptable clinical benefit-risk balance for the patient and objective. Explain why an apparently improved monitor result can lead to stopping a proposed drug rather than continuing it. Do not extrapolate the harm estimate unchanged to every structurally normal patient, but preserve the clear safety boundary in an infarct-scar substrate.')],['CAST','ESC2022'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Exercise-Associated Ventricular Arrhythmia',
 'During supervised exercise testing, a patient with unexplained exertional presyncope develops increasing multifocal PVCs and polymorphic ventricular runs as workload rises. The resting ECG and initial echo are unrevealing. Which interpretation is most appropriate?',
 'The exercise response is concerning and warrants evaluation for underlying myocardial or inherited electrical disease',[
 ('The normal resting studies establish benign idiopathic ectopy despite the exercise response','Normal initial tests reduce some concerns but do not exclude concealed cardiomyopathy or adrenergically triggered electrical disease. Symptoms and increasing complex ventricular arrhythmia change the assessment.'),
 ('The pattern establishes obstructive coronary disease as the cause','Ischemia is a relevant possibility depending on age, symptoms and risk factors, but exercise-associated polymorphic arrhythmia can also occur with CPVT and other substrates. The pattern alone does not establish coronary obstruction.'),
 ('The pattern establishes CPVT without further assessment of structural or secondary causes','CPVT is an important consideration, especially with a normal resting ECG and adrenergic bidirectional or polymorphic VT. Diagnosis still requires evaluation of the full phenotype and exclusion of relevant alternative causes.')],
 [S('Interpret Complexity and Symptoms Alongside Exercise Response','**Increasing ventricular arrhythmia with exercise, particularly polymorphic runs and presyncope, is a red flag rather than a diagnosis by itself.** Stop the test and assess rhythm and perfusion according to the clinical situation.',
    'Document the twelve-lead morphology, workload, symptom relationship and recovery behavior. A single isolated PVC during exercise has a different implication from progressively complex symptomatic arrhythmia.',
    'Review family history, medications, electrolytes and prior events. Syncope described as a seizure or unexplained drowning in relatives can be relevant to an inherited assessment.'),
  S('Investigate the Likely Substrate Before Reassurance','**Use coronary assessment, CMR and inherited-arrhythmia evaluation selectively according to the phenotype.** Normal resting echo does not exclude early cardiomyopathy, and a normal baseline ECG is compatible with CPVT.',
    'An outflow focus can also be adrenergically sensitive, so exercise provocation does not automatically establish a malignant inherited syndrome. Morphology, complexity, symptoms and the wider evaluation determine the distinction.',
    'Avoid unsupervised high-intensity exertion while a concerning presentation is being investigated, with individualized advice from the treating team. A reduction of ectopy at one later workload or in recovery does not independently prove safety. Conversely, exercise testing is not a universal prohibition on activity for every person with PVCs; many patients with an adequately characterized idiopathic focus can receive a tailored plan. Repeat or extended monitoring may clarify variable burden, but it does not replace a substrate investigation when clinical red flags are present. Treatment and family evaluation follow the established disease, not an isolated stress-test label.')],['ESC2022','CMP2023'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Clinical Diagnosis of Long-QT Syndrome',
 'An asymptomatic young adult has manually confirmed QTc values of 490–500 ms on repeated twelve-lead ECGs. QRS is narrow, electrolytes are normal and no secondary drug or systemic cause is identified. Under ESC 2022 criteria, which conclusion is appropriate?',
 'The repeated QTc meets a clinical diagnostic criterion for long-QT syndrome',[
 ('Diagnosis requires prior syncope even with this repeated QTc','Symptoms affect risk and treatment, but ESC allows diagnosis with repeated QTc at least 480 ms with or without symptoms when the measurement and context are appropriate.'),
 ('Diagnosis requires a pathogenic genetic result before clinical criteria can be applied','Genetic testing and counseling are recommended in clinically diagnosed disease, but a negative or unavailable result does not negate a qualifying clinical phenotype.'),
 ('Diagnosis requires QTc at least 500 ms on every tracing','That imports a different threshold into the named ESC framework. Repeated QTc at least 480 ms is a qualifying criterion; measurement quality and exclusion of secondary causes remain essential.')],
 [S('Use the Named Diagnostic Framework','**ESC 2022 recommends diagnosing LQTS with repeated QTc at least 480 ms or an LQTS diagnostic score greater than three.** The score integrates electrical, clinical and family evidence rather than QT duration alone.',
    'With arrhythmic syncope and no secondary explanation, repeated QTc 460 to below 480 ms warrants considering the diagnosis. This more conditional recommendation should not be confused with the stronger repeated-480-ms criterion.',
    'A pathogenic causal variant can establish a molecular diagnosis despite a normal QT phenotype. Reduced penetrance means that one normal ECG does not exclude inherited disease in an appropriately evaluated family.'),
  S('Verify Repolarization Before Assigning a Lifelong Diagnosis','**Confirm the end of the T wave and inspect rate correction, rhythm and QRS width.** Automated QTc can be misleading with unusual T–U morphology, tachycardia or broad ventricular activation.',
    'Exclude drugs, electrolyte disturbance, bradycardia-associated changes and acute systemic or cardiac illness. Reassess after recovery when a transient setting, such as immediately after cardiac arrest, can distort repolarization.',
    'Arrange genetic counseling, phenotype-directed testing and family evaluation after a supported diagnosis. A variant of uncertain significance is not equivalent to a pathogenic result. Risk assessment includes QT magnitude, genotype, symptoms and prior arrest; diagnostic eligibility alone does not mean every patient needs an ICD. Avoid QT-prolonging medicines, correct electrolyte problems and discuss an appropriate preventive strategy, commonly including a preferred nonselective beta blocker. Exercise-recovery ECG can contribute to specialist assessment, but indiscriminate provocative testing is not a substitute for reliable repeated ECGs and clinical interpretation. Document the framework and measurement rather than preserving an unsupported automated label.'),
  S('Calculate the Modified Diagnostic Score Consistently','**Apply the score without secondary causes affecting the ECG.** Its QT correction uses Bazett: QTc = QT divided by the square root of RR, with both intervals measured in seconds.',
    'Choose the applicable resting-QTc category rather than adding mutually exclusive QT ranges. Torsades and syncope are mutually exclusive score entries; do not use the same relative for both family-history entries.',
    'Totals at most 1 indicate low probability, 1.5–3 intermediate probability, and greater than 3 support clinical diagnosis. This diagnostic score is not a numerical ICD implantation rule.')],['ESC2022'],style='direct',
 table=(['Modified LQTS score component','Points'],[
 ['Resting QTc ≥480 ms / 460–479 ms / male QTc 450–459 ms','3.5 / 2 / 1'],
 ['QTc ≥480 ms at minute 4 of postexercise recovery','1'],
 ['Documented torsades','2'],['Alternating T-wave morphology','1'],['T-wave notches involving ≥3 leads','1'],
 ['Resting rate below the age-specific second percentile','0.5'],
 ['Syncope associated with stress / syncope without stress','2 / 1'],['Deafness present from birth','0.5'],
 ['Relative with an established clinical or molecular LQTS diagnosis','1'],
 ['Unexplained death before age 30 in a first-degree relative','0.5'],
 ['Disease-causing variant in an established LQTS gene','3.5']]))

R['LQTGENE']=('Long QT Syndrome Overview, GeneReviews','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK1129/')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Common Long-QT Genotypes and Trigger Patterns',
 'In a family with confirmed long-QT syndrome, several events occur during swimming or vigorous exercise. Which common genotype-mechanism pairing most closely matches this trigger association?',
 'KCNQ1-related LQT1 with reduced slow delayed-rectifier potassium current',[
 ('KCNH2-related LQT2 with reduced rapid delayed-rectifier potassium current','This pairing is mechanistically correct, but sudden auditory stimuli and emotional arousal are particularly characteristic associations. Exercise can still trigger events, so the distinction is a tendency rather than an exclusion rule.'),
 ('SCN5A-related LQT3 with increased late sodium current','This is a valid LQT3 mechanism, but events are more characteristically associated with rest or sleep and slower rates. The strong swimming/exertional pattern favors LQT1.'),
 ('RYR2-related CPVT with abnormal intracellular calcium release','RYR2-associated CPVT can cause exercise-related syncope and polymorphic or bidirectional VT, usually with a normal resting QT. The stem already establishes long-QT disease rather than an unexplained adrenergic arrhythmia.')],
 [S('Relate the Channel Defect to the Typical Trigger','**KCNQ1-related LQT1 reduces IKs, the slow delayed-rectifier potassium current.** Exercise and swimming are characteristic event settings, reflecting impaired adaptation of repolarization during adrenergic stress.',
    'KCNH2-related LQT2 reduces IKr; abrupt auditory stimuli and emotional arousal are useful associations. SCN5A-related LQT3 commonly increases persistent late sodium current and has a stronger rest/sleep association.',
    'These are genotype-associated patterns, not deterministic rules. A trigger cannot replace molecular testing, and an atypical trigger does not invalidate an established genotype.'),
  S('Use Genotype to Refine Rather Than Replace Clinical Care','**QT magnitude, prior symptoms, age, sex and treatment adherence remain important alongside genotype.** A normal or mildly prolonged QT can occur in a genetically affected relative because penetrance is incomplete.',
    'Nonselective beta blockers such as nadolol or propranolol are preferred preventive agents in suitable patients. Selected LQT3 patients can benefit from late-sodium-current inhibition with mexiletine under specialist assessment and response verification.',
    'Provide individualized activity and water-safety advice and a clear emergency plan. Avoid QT-prolonging drugs and correct electrolyte depletion regardless of the genotype, rather than assuming a trigger-specific lifestyle change is sufficient protection. Family testing should follow a pathogenic or likely pathogenic result interpreted with counseling; uncertain variants do not establish predictive diagnoses. Distinguish SCN5A gain-of-function long-QT phenotypes from other SCN5A-associated disorders, including loss-of-function Brugada disease. The gene name alone does not specify the functional effect or management. CPVT remains an important alternative when exercise-induced arrhythmia occurs without QT prolongation, but it is not the best pairing for this confirmed LQTS phenotype.')],['ESC2022','LQTGENE'],
 table=(['Syndrome','Common gene/current','Characteristic association'],[
 ['LQT1','KCNQ1; reduced IKs','Exercise, particularly swimming'],['LQT2','KCNH2; reduced IKr','Auditory startle, emotional arousal'],['LQT3','SCN5A; increased late INa','Rest or sleep']]))

R['DRUG2025']=('EHRA practical antiarrhythmic drug compendium, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1093/europace/euaf076')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Preventive Drug Treatment in Congenital Long-QT Syndrome',
 'A young adult with confirmed LQT1, prolonged QT and prior exertional syncope has no bronchospasm, significant bradycardia or AV block. Which preventive drug strategy best matches ESC guidance?',
 'Use an appropriately titrated preferred nonselective beta blocker such as nadolol or propranolol',[
 ('Use metoprolol as an equivalent preferred choice irrespective of genotype and prior symptoms','Metoprolol can have clinical uses, but comparative evidence and ESC guidance favor nadolol or propranolol for LQTS prevention. Beta blockers should not be assumed interchangeable for this inherited syndrome.'),
 ('Use mexiletine as the routine first medication for LQT1','Mexiletine targets late sodium current and has an established genotype-directed role in LQT3 with prolonged QT. It does not replace the preferred beta-blocker approach for this supplied LQT1 presentation.'),
 ('Use sotalol to combine beta blockade with an additional class III effect','Sotalol prolongs repolarization and can provoke torsades. Its beta-blocking property does not make it an appropriate preventive substitute in congenital long-QT disease.')],
 [S('Reduce Adrenergic Risk With the Appropriate Agent','**Nadolol and propranolol are preferred beta blockers for congenital LQTS with documented QT prolongation.** Their clinical role is prevention of arrhythmic events, not merely lowering an office pulse.',
    'Titrate to the patient’s age, weight, renal function, symptoms and tolerance, with specialist review of exercise response when appropriate. Nadolol requires particular attention to renal clearance; propranolol formulation and dosing frequency affect adherence.',
    'The EHRA 2025 compendium lists adult oral ranges of nadolol 40–120 mg/day and propranolol 80–320 mg/day, with slow-release propranolol preferred. These reference ranges do not replace individualized initiation or pediatric weight-based prescribing.'),
  S('Add Genotype-Specific and Nonpharmacological Care','**Mexiletine is indicated in LQT3 with prolonged QT under ESC 2022 guidance.** Assess the mutation/phenotype, QT response, conduction and adverse effects rather than prescribing it to every prolonged-QT patient.',
    'The relationship between beta blockade and mexiletine in LQT3 needs individualized specialist judgment; evidence does not justify claiming that every patient needs an identical combination. Emerging use in other selected genotypes should be distinguished from the core LQT3 recommendation.',
    'Avoid QT-prolonging medicines, correct electrolyte losses and provide genotype-informed activity and emergency advice. Check adherence before labeling a breakthrough event drug failure, and avoid abrupt withdrawal. Recurrent arrhythmic symptoms despite adequate treatment require escalation assessment, including ICD or left cardiac sympathetic denervation in the appropriate setting. A tolerated dose that lowers resting heart rate does not alone prove effective protection. Explain adverse effects and the plan for illness, vomiting or new interacting medicines so prevention remains reliable outside the clinic.')],['ESC2022','DRUG2025'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'ICD and Sympathetic Denervation in High-Risk LQTS',
 'A patient with congenital LQTS survives documented VF without a reversible trigger. The patient has good expected functional survival and is receiving appropriate syndrome-directed medication. Which long-term strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Add an ICD for secondary prevention while continuing appropriate medical and trigger-directed treatment',[
 ('Use medication alone because QT shortening removes the significance of prior VF','Medication reduces risk but does not erase a documented arrest in this inherited substrate. Apparent QT improvement is not sufficient evidence to omit secondary-prevention protection in the described eligible patient.'),
 ('Replace all medical treatment with ICD implantation','An ICD terminates dangerous episodes but does not prevent every arrhythmia or remove adrenergic triggers. Continued syndrome-directed treatment can reduce recurrence and painful shocks.'),
 ('Choose left cardiac sympathetic denervation as a universally equivalent substitute for an acceptable ICD','LCSD has an important role when ICD therapy is contraindicated or declined and in selected refractory patients. It is not a universal equivalent replacement for an appropriate accepted secondary-prevention ICD.')],
 [S('Prior Arrest Defines a Different Risk Setting','**An eligible LQTS survivor of cardiac arrest requires secondary-prevention ICD assessment alongside effective medical treatment.** The decision differs from prophylactic implantation in an asymptomatic genotype-positive relative.',
    'Confirm the arrest rhythm, evaluate reversible contributors and optimize beta blockade and genotype-directed treatment. Assess expected survival, device-related burdens, age and patient preferences.',
    'A device treats episodes after they begin. Good adherence, avoidance of QT-prolonging drugs and correction of electrolyte abnormalities remain essential to reduce arrhythmia and shock burden.'),
  S('Use LCSD for the Appropriate Escalation Problem','**ESC identifies LCSD for symptomatic LQTS when ICD treatment is contraindicated or declined, or for recurrent arrhythmic syncope or multiple shocks despite appropriate treatment in an ICD carrier.** It reduces sympathetic influence without guaranteeing elimination of all risk.',
    'When effective-dose medication is not tolerated or is contraindicated in a symptomatic patient, ICD or LCSD can be considered through specialist assessment. The indication should reflect the actual treatment limitation rather than a generic wish to avoid follow-up.',
    'Reassess whether events are arrhythmic and whether medication, dose, adherence or interacting drugs explain apparent treatment failure. Device programming should minimize avoidable therapy while retaining dependable rescue. Discuss procedural and long-term complications of both interventions, including repeat treatment and surveillance. Asymptomatic high-risk patients need individualized primary-prevention assessment rather than automatic extrapolation from this arrest survivor. EPS inducibility is not the routine method for deciding LQTS risk. Explain the complementary purposes of medication, denervation and an ICD so a technically successful intervention is not misinterpreted as permission to abandon the remaining prevention plan.')],['ESC2022','STORM2024'])

R['GENETICS2022']=('EHRA/HRS/APHRS/LAHRS genetic testing consensus, 2022','https://doi.org/10.1002/joa3.12717')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Type 1 Brugada ECG Pattern',
 'A young adult has a spontaneous ECG showing coved ST elevation of 3 mm followed by T-wave inversion in V1 recorded in the third intercostal space. Directed evaluation finds no explanatory structural disease, ischemia or electrolyte abnormality. Which interpretation is appropriate?',
 'This is a type 1 Brugada pattern; a high right-precordial lead can document the finding',[
 ('The finding is nondiagnostic because both V1 and V2 must show the pattern','A qualifying type 1 pattern in at least one of V1 or V2 can meet the ECG criterion. Requiring both leads can miss a genuine finding.'),
 ('The finding is nondiagnostic because only fourth-intercostal recordings are accepted','High right-precordial recordings in the second or third intercostal space can reveal the pattern. Their use is deliberate rather than an automatic reason to reject the tracing.'),
 ('The finding represents a type 2 saddleback pattern','Type 1 has a coved descending ST segment followed by a negative T wave. A saddleback configuration is a different pattern and is not independently equivalent to type 1.')],
 [S('Recognize the Morphology and Lead Position','**Type 1 Brugada ECG has coved right-precordial ST elevation of at least 2 mm, followed by T-wave inversion, in at least one of V1 or V2.** Standard fourth or high second-/third-intercostal lead positions are relevant.',
    'Confirm calibration: 2 mm at standard gain corresponds to 0.2 mV. Do not reproduce a unit transcription error that would turn this into a 2-mV threshold.',
    'A saddleback type 2 appearance raises a different diagnostic question. It should not be treated as a standalone type 1 result or an automatic device indication.'),
  S('Separate Pattern Confirmation From Clinical Risk','**A spontaneous type 1 pattern without another explanatory heart disease supports Brugada syndrome under ESC assessment.** Exclude phenocopies such as important metabolic disturbances or ischemic and structural mimics.',
    'An induced type 1 pattern has lower specificity and requires additional clinical context under ESC 2022, such as documented polymorphic VT/VF, arrhythmic syncope or relevant family history. Drug challenge requires an expert protocol and appropriate indication.',
    'Fever can unmask genuine susceptibility and should not automatically be dismissed as a benign phenocopy. Treat fever promptly, review sodium-channel-blocking drugs and assess symptoms and family history. Genetic counseling and appropriate SCN5A testing can assist evaluation, but a negative result does not exclude the clinical syndrome. The ECG finding establishes a diagnostic pathway, while prior arrest, arrhythmic symptoms and spontaneous pattern guide risk assessment. An asymptomatic patient with a pattern is not automatically managed like a survivor of VF; avoid equating recognition of the tracing with a universal ICD prescription.')],['ESC2022','GENETICS2022'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Brugada Risk Assessment Beyond the Genetic Result',
 'A patient with a spontaneous type 1 Brugada pattern has abrupt syncope at rest without a vagal prodrome. Expert assessment considers the event likely arrhythmic. SCN5A testing is negative. Which next interpretation is most appropriate?',
 'The negative genetic test does not negate the clinical risk; assess ICD protection based on the spontaneous pattern and likely arrhythmic syncope',[
 ('The negative SCN5A result excludes Brugada syndrome and removes the need for risk assessment','Only a minority of clinically affected patients have an identifiable causal SCN5A variant. Negative testing cannot overrule a supported clinical phenotype and concerning event.'),
 ('The syncope can be classified as low risk because resting ventricular function is normal','Primary electrical syndromes can cause dangerous arrhythmia with preserved ventricular function. A normal EF does not neutralize likely arrhythmic syncope in the supplied phenotype.'),
 ('ICD assessment should wait until a sodium-channel-blocker challenge reproduces the spontaneous pattern','A diagnostic spontaneous type 1 pattern is already present. Repeat pharmacological provocation is not a prerequisite for acting on the supplied clinical risk and can add unnecessary testing.')],
 [S('Clinical Events Remain Central','**Prior cardiac arrest or documented sustained ventricular arrhythmia creates a high-risk Brugada setting.** A spontaneous type 1 pattern with likely arrhythmic syncope supports consideration of ICD implantation under ESC guidance.',
    'Differentiate arrhythmic syncope from reflex syncope through circumstances, prodrome, witness history and recovery. A Brugada ECG does not make every faint arrhythmic, so careful classification matters before a lifelong intervention.',
    'An implantable loop recorder can help selected unexplained-syncope cases when the mechanism remains uncertain. It does not replace indicated protection after documented VF.'),
  S('Use Genetics for Its Actual Purpose','**SCN5A testing identifies only a minority of clinically diagnosed Brugada cases.** A negative result does not exclude the syndrome, and a variant of uncertain significance does not establish a causal diagnosis.',
    'A pathogenic familial result can support cascade evaluation with counseling. Genotype alone should not be treated as a universal ICD criterion without phenotype and clinical risk assessment.',
    'Asymptomatic patients, particularly those with only a drug-induced pattern, have a different balance from the symptomatic spontaneous-pattern presentation here. Programmed stimulation has a selected role in some risk discussions, but inducibility is not a universally decisive test. Counsel on prompt fever treatment, avoidance of relevant drugs and alcohol intoxication, and urgent reassessment after syncope. Discuss device complications and expected benefit alongside the arrhythmic risk. Continue follow-up and family evaluation even when no molecular cause is found, because an unavailable genetic explanation does not make a demonstrated electrical phenotype disappear.')],['ESC2022','GENETICS2022'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Electrical Storm in Brugada Syndrome',
 'A patient with established Brugada syndrome develops fever and recurrent VF terminated by appropriate ICD shocks. Electrolytes are corrected and no acute coronary cause is identified. Which syndrome-directed acute strategy is appropriate in expert monitored care?',
 'Treat fever promptly and consider isoproterenol infusion to suppress recurrent VF',[
 ('Escalate beta blockade as for scar-related monomorphic VT storm','Beta blockade is important in structural-heart-disease storm and CPVT, but those mechanisms differ. Bradycardia and increased vagal influence can be unfavorable in Brugada disease; the syndrome-directed acute approach includes isoproterenol.'),
 ('Give flecainide to suppress the recurrent ventricular arrhythmia','Flecainide has selected AF and CPVT uses, but sodium-channel blockade can aggravate the Brugada phenotype. Its use as a diagnostic challenge in a controlled setting does not make it a treatment for Brugada storm.'),
 ('Give magnesium as the sole mechanism-directed therapy because VF is polymorphic','Magnesium treats long-QT torsades or deficiency. It does not replace fever control and Brugada-specific management when recurrent VF occurs without that prolonged-QT mechanism.')],
 [S('Treat the Trigger and the Specific Electrical Substrate','**Fever can unmask or aggravate Brugada electrical instability.** Give antipyretic treatment, assess the illness and remove relevant provoking drugs while maintaining immediate defibrillation capability.',
    'Isoproterenol can suppress Brugada electrical storm through adrenergic effects that improve the relevant electrical balance. Use a titrated infusion with expert ECG and hemodynamic monitoring rather than extrapolating an unmonitored fixed regimen.',
    'This is a mechanism-specific exception: catecholaminergic stimulation can worsen CPVT, and the same drug must not be generalized to every inherited ventricular syndrome.'),
  S('Plan Prevention Beyond the Acute Infusion','**Quinidine can reduce recurrent VF or appropriate ICD shocks in selected Brugada patients.** Monitor QT, conduction, interactions, gastrointestinal intolerance and other adverse effects; the drug has a distinct role despite its repolarization effects.',
    'Recurrent shocks despite appropriate treatment or drug intolerance can warrant specialist substrate-ablation assessment. Such procedures require expertise and do not justify abandoning independently indicated ICD protection.',
    'Interrogate the device to confirm VF and appropriate therapy, and reassess the entire clinical state rather than assuming every shock represents the same event. Shock-related distress still needs treatment, but a generic scar-storm drug sequence can be inappropriate for this substrate. Continue counseling on prompt fever management, avoidance of relevant sodium-channel-blocking drugs and alcohol intoxication, and urgent review after syncope. Treat the infection or other fever source concurrently. A transiently normalized ECG after defervescence does not erase established Brugada susceptibility or a history of VF. Review family evaluation and the durable prevention plan after stabilization, distinguishing suppression of the current storm from elimination of future arrhythmic risk.')],['ESC2022','STORM2024','DRUG2025'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Recognition of Catecholaminergic Polymorphic VT',
 'A teenager has recurrent exercise-related syncope. Resting ECG, including QTc, and structural imaging are normal. Supervised exercise produces ventricular ectopy progressing to bidirectional and polymorphic VT as adrenergic stimulation increases. No relevant drug exposure is found. Which diagnosis is most likely?',
 'Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia',[
 ('KCNQ1-related long-QT syndrome','LQT1 commonly causes exertional or swimming-related events and can have reduced penetrance. Here the characteristic adrenergic bidirectional progression with normal resting repolarization favors CPVT, while a full inherited assessment remains appropriate.'),
 ('Idiopathic RVOT tachycardia','Outflow VT can be exercise sensitive in an otherwise normal heart, but it is usually a monomorphic focal rhythm. Progressive bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular activity is a stronger CPVT clue.'),
 ('Digoxin-induced bidirectional VT','Digoxin toxicity can produce bidirectional VT and is an essential acquired differential. The absence of exposure and the reproducible exertional phenotype in this young patient favor an inherited calcium-handling disorder.')],
 [S('A Normal Resting ECG Does Not Exclude Dangerous Inherited Disease','**CPVT is characterized by adrenergically provoked bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular arrhythmia in a structurally normal heart.** Exertional or emotion-related syncope can be misclassified as epilepsy when the rhythm has not been recorded.',
    'Exercise can produce increasing ectopy, couplets and more complex runs as heart rate rises. The precise sequence varies, and a single negative exercise study does not exclude every genetically affected patient.',
    'Assess personal and family histories, acquired triggers and alternative cardiac disease. Normal resting QT helps distinguish the usual phenotype from LQTS but is not a complete inherited-disease evaluation by itself.'),
  S('Connect the Phenotype to Calcium Handling and Prevention','**RYR2-associated disease commonly involves abnormal sarcoplasmic-reticulum calcium release and delayed afterdepolarization-mediated triggered activity.** CASQ2 and other validated genes account for additional phenotypes with different inheritance patterns.',
    'Arrange genetic counseling and targeted family evaluation rather than treating a variant of uncertain significance as a confirmed cause. Clinical diagnosis can remain meaningful when testing does not identify a pathogenic variant.',
    'Nonselective beta blockade, usually nadolol or propranolol, and individualized restriction of strenuous adrenergic triggers form the preventive foundation. Flecainide and selected denervation or device strategies are considered when control is inadequate. Exercise reassessment can help monitor response, but apparent suppression at one visit does not justify stopping medication. Catecholamines used to suppress Brugada storm can worsen CPVT, illustrating why polymorphic rhythms require a mechanism-based treatment plan. A survivor of arrest needs specialist secondary-prevention assessment with optimized medication because painful ICD shocks can increase adrenergic drive. Provide a family emergency plan and continuing follow-up rather than reassurance from normal resting imaging alone.')],['ESC2022','GENETICS2022','STORM2024'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Escalation of Treatment in CPVT',
 'A patient with confirmed CPVT remains adherent to the highest tolerated nadolol dose but has persistent exercise-induced ventricular ectopy and bidirectional runs on supervised reassessment. There has been no cardiac arrest. Which next pharmacological step is guideline-supported?',
 'Add flecainide with specialist ECG and exercise-response monitoring',[
 ('Replace nadolol with amiodarone as the standard next strategy','Amiodarone has important uses in other ventricular substrates, but it is not the established next preventive strategy for adrenergic CPVT persisting despite appropriate beta blockade.'),
 ('Add isoproterenol to prevent the exercise-associated episodes','Isoproterenol can suppress Brugada storm or selected acquired pause-dependent torsades. Adrenergic stimulation can worsen CPVT and is not a preventive adjunct for this calcium-handling syndrome.'),
 ('Implant an ICD and discontinue beta blockade to avoid drug adverse effects','An ICD can be indicated for arrest survivors or selected refractory disease, but it does not replace medical suppression. Painful shocks can amplify adrenergic drive, so withdrawing preventive treatment can worsen the clinical problem.')],
 [S('Optimize the Foundation Before Escalating','**Preferred nonselective beta blockade is the preventive foundation for clinically expressed CPVT.** Confirm adherence, dose, tolerance and the context of breakthrough events rather than interpreting one recurrence as proof that beta blockers have no role.',
    'Exercise testing can assess residual adrenergic arrhythmia under supervised conditions. Review renal function for nadolol and assess excessive bradycardia or other limits to titration.',
    'Provide individualized activity advice, avoidance of strenuous triggers and an emergency plan. A quiet resting ECG is expected in many affected patients and does not demonstrate adequate protection during stress.'),
  S('Add Mechanism-Appropriate Treatment and Reassess','**ESC 2022 supports adding flecainide for recurrent syncope, polymorphic/bidirectional VT or persistent exertional PVCs despite the highest tolerated beta-blocker dose.** Check QRS and conduction, renal/hepatic status, interactions and the response to treatment.',
    'The use of flecainide here must not be confused with its hazard in post-MI scar. The underlying disease and purpose differ, so the drug cannot be classified as universally appropriate or universally inappropriate for every ventricular arrhythmia.',
    'LCSD should be considered when therapeutic beta-blocker/flecainide treatment is ineffective, contraindicated or not tolerated. An ICD is recommended with optimized syndrome-directed medication after cardiac arrest and can be considered for selected persistent arrhythmic syncope or bidirectional/polymorphic VT despite combination therapy. Program devices carefully and minimize avoidable shocks because catecholamine surges can perpetuate arrhythmia. These interventions complement rather than replace suppression of the adrenergic substrate. Follow symptoms and exercise response over time, revisit adherence and provide genetic counseling and family assessment. Do not use programmed stimulation as the routine sudden-death risk test for CPVT or stop therapy because a single follow-up exercise study improves.')],['ESC2022','STORM2024','DRUG2025'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Early Repolarization Pattern Versus Arrhythmic Syndrome',
 'An asymptomatic adult has stable terminal QRS notching with 1.5-mm J-point elevation in two contiguous inferior leads. There is no syncope, premature familial sudden death or documented ventricular arrhythmia, and directed assessment is reassuring. Which interpretation is most appropriate?',
 'An early-repolarization ECG pattern is present; the tracing alone does not establish early-repolarization syndrome or an ICD indication',[
 ('Early-repolarization syndrome is established by the inferior-lead distribution alone','Inferior distribution can contribute to risk assessment, but a pattern is not the same as a clinical arrhythmic syndrome. The ECG finding alone does not supply the missing ventricular event.'),
 ('A type 1 Brugada pattern is established because J-point elevation exceeds one millimeter','Brugada type 1 requires a specific coved right-precordial morphology, not generic inferior J-point elevation. Lead location and morphology distinguish the patterns.'),
 ('Short-QT syndrome is established by terminal QRS notching','Short-QT syndrome concerns abbreviated repolarization assessed through QT and clinical context. Terminal QRS notching does not establish a short QT or its associated syndrome.')],
 [S('Define the Electrical Pattern Without Overdiagnosis','**Early repolarization includes terminal QRS notching or slurring with J-point elevation in inferior or lateral leads.** ESC describes elevation of at least 1 mm in at least two adjacent inferior and/or lateral leads.',
    'Confirm that the deflection belongs to terminal ventricular activation rather than artefact or a different abnormality. ST elevation is not required by the dedicated pattern consensus, so an elevated ST segment alone should not replace the actual J-point assessment.',
    'The pattern occurs in many healthy people. Some morphologies and clinical contexts carry greater concern, but population associations do not independently establish a dangerous syndrome in every individual.'),
  S('Require the Clinical Phenotype for Syndrome Assessment','**ESC diagnoses early-repolarization syndrome in an appropriately evaluated survivor of polymorphic VT or VF with this pattern and no other explanatory heart disease.** Such a survivor requires secondary-prevention assessment rather than reassurance based on a familiar ECG variant.',
    'An asymptomatic person with a reassuring history has a different management balance. Do not implant an ICD or prescribe suppressive quinidine solely because an incidental pattern is present.',
    'Short-QT disease is another separate diagnosis: verify QT measurement, rate behavior and acquired causes, then integrate symptoms, family history and genetic evidence. ESC advises considering SQTS with QTc 320–360 ms and arrhythmic syncope; this conditional framework should not be reduced to a generic short-looking ST segment. Early AF or familial VF can be relevant clues. For either syndrome, avoid interpreting a genetic variant of uncertain significance as proof. New syncope or a resuscitated event warrants renewed comprehensive assessment even if a previous incidental ECG had been reassuring. Explain the distinction between an ECG descriptor and a disease associated with documented ventricular arrhythmia so follow-up is proportionate to the actual evidence.')],['ESC2022','GENETICS2022'],
 table=(['Finding','What is needed for interpretation'],[
 ['Inferolateral J-point notch/slur','Pattern assessment plus symptoms and family history'],['Type 1 Brugada','Coved right-precordial morphology and clinical context'],['Short QT','Reliable QT measurement, secondary-cause exclusion and phenotype assessment']]))

R['RARE2026']=('EHRA/ACNAP rare primary arrhythmia syndromes clinical consensus, 2026','https://doi.org/10.1093/europace/euag184')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Short-QT Syndrome: Diagnostic Context and Prevention',
 'An adult with manually confirmed QTc 350 ms on repeated ECGs survives documented VF. Expert evaluation finds no structural heart disease, ischemia, electrolyte disturbance or relevant drug exposure. Under the ESC 2022 framework, which interpretation is best supported?',
 'Short-QT syndrome is supported by QTc at most 360 ms combined with otherwise unexplained cardiac arrest',[
 ('The QTc must be at most 320 ms even after otherwise unexplained VF','A very short QT supplies a separate diagnostic consideration, but ESC also recognizes QTc at most 360 ms with qualifying clinical or familial evidence. The arrest is decisive additional context here.'),
 ('A pathogenic molecular result is required before the short-QT phenotype can be diagnosed','A pathogenic variant is one qualifying route, not a prerequisite in every patient. An otherwise unexplained resuscitated arrest with the supplied QT phenotype can support diagnosis without a positive genetic result.'),
 ('Any repeated QTc at most 360 ms establishes the same diagnosis in an asymptomatic person','QT shortening overlaps with physiological variation and acquired causes. At this threshold, symptoms, family history or molecular evidence matter; the result cannot be transferred unchanged to an asymptomatic incidental finding.')],
 [S('Identify Persistent Abbreviated Repolarization','**ESC 2022 supports SQTS diagnosis with QTc at most 360 ms and a pathogenic variant, a family history of SQTS, or survival of cardiac arrest without explanatory heart disease.** Name the framework because older consensus thresholds differ.',
    'The same guideline advises considering SQTS when QTc is at most 320 ms, and when QTc is 320–360 ms with arrhythmic syncope. These consideration pathways should not be conflated with proving a malignant syndrome from every borderline measurement.',
    'Measure QT manually on repeated recordings at interpretable rates. Rate correction can mislead at heart-rate extremes; review the uncorrected interval, rate behavior and T-wave morphology rather than relying on one automated number.'),
  S('Exclude Acquired Shortening and Interpret Genetics Carefully','**Check calcium, potassium, temperature, acid–base status and medication exposure.** Hypercalcemia, hyperkalemia, hyperthermia and drugs such as digoxin can shorten repolarization and require their own treatment.',
    'A structurally normal heart is compatible with an inherited electrical disorder. Early atrial fibrillation and familial ventricular events can be clues, but neither replaces careful evaluation of the arrest and alternative explanations.',
    'Arrange specialist genetic counseling and phenotype-directed testing. A negative panel does not exclude a supported clinical diagnosis, and a variant of uncertain significance must not be promoted into causal proof or predictive testing of healthy relatives.'),
  S('Separate Secondary Prevention From Uncertain Primary Prevention','**A survivor of otherwise unexplained VF needs ICD-based secondary-prevention assessment, with attention to device sensing and inappropriate shocks.** Tall T waves and a short repolarization interval can create sensing challenges.',
    'Primary prevention in an asymptomatic short-QT phenotype is less certain and requires individual risk assessment. Programmed ventricular stimulation is not recommended as a routine sudden-death risk-stratification method for SQTS.',
    'Quinidine has the strongest supporting drug evidence, principally from small cohorts rather than large mortality trials. ESC 2022 permits consideration when an indicated ICD is contraindicated or declined, and in selected asymptomatic familial-risk cases. Monitor ECG, QT prolongation, interactions and adverse effects; QT lengthening alone does not establish equivalent protection to an indicated ICD. Drug use and dosing require specialist and formulation-specific planning. Continue clinical follow-up and appropriate relative evaluation rather than treating one improved ECG as elimination of inherited risk.'),
  S('Interpret the 2026 Rare-Arrhythmia Consensus','**The EHRA/ACNAP 2026 statement advises quinidine as the initial pharmacological treatment for symptomatic pediatric SQTS.** This is a pediatric medication recommendation, not a replacement for secondary-prevention device assessment in this adult arrest survivor.',
    'It identifies uncertainty about preventive quinidine in asymptomatic SQTS, advises against active treatment based solely on genotype when QTc is normal, and supports ECG-based first-degree-relative screening. Selected asymptomatic patients may benefit from a loop recorder.',
    'Its diagnostic discussion retains the very-short-QT and qualifying-context pathways. Keep the year and population explicit rather than merging this consensus with older threshold rules or presenting uncertain asymptomatic treatment as an established mandate.')],['ESC2022','GENETICS2022','DRUG2025','RARE2026'])

R['HFUS2022']=('AHA/ACC/HFSA heart failure guideline, 2022','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2021.12.012')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Secondary-Prevention ICD After Resuscitated VF',
 'A patient with a remote MI survives documented VF. There is no acute infarction, culprit ischemia, drug toxicity or electrolyte explanation after evaluation. LVEF is 42%, and meaningful functional survival beyond one year is expected. Which strategy is appropriate?',
 'Offer an ICD for secondary prevention after shared assessment of benefits and burdens',[
 ('Defer ICD because EF is above the usual primary-prevention threshold','Primary-prevention EF cut-offs do not exclude an eligible survivor of VF without a reversible cause. A prior malignant event supplies a different indication.'),
 ('Require inducible sustained VT at EPS before offering an ICD','EPS has selected diagnostic and risk-assessment uses, but documented VF without a reversible cause does not require an additional inducibility result to establish the secondary-prevention pathway.'),
 ('Use chronic amiodarone as an equivalent substitute for ICD protection','Amiodarone can reduce recurrent ventricular arrhythmia in selected patients but is not an equivalent routine substitute for indicated secondary-prevention ICD therapy. Drug and device roles are complementary.')],
 [S('Distinguish a Prior Event From Predicted Future Risk','**An eligible survivor of VF or hemodynamically poorly tolerated VT without a reversible cause has a secondary-prevention ICD indication.** The EF thresholds used to select event-free primary-prevention patients answer a different question.',
    'Confirm the rhythm and investigate acute ischemia, infarction, electrolyte abnormalities, proarrhythmic medication and other potentially reversible contributors. A single abnormal laboratory value does not automatically prove that it fully explains the event.',
    'Remote infarct scar remains an important persistent substrate even when global EF is above thirty-five percent. Preserved or mildly reduced EF does not erase the significance of documented VF.'),
  S('Assess Reversibility and Expected Benefit Carefully','**Consider functional survival, competing disease, device complications and patient preferences before implantation.** The device can terminate future ventricular arrhythmia but does not treat HF progression or prevent every cause of death.',
    'VT/VF during the early acute-MI period has a different assessment from late scar-related events. Do not apply a primary-prevention waiting interval as a blanket reason to delay every secondary-prevention indication.',
    'Continue coronary and HF treatment and consider antiarrhythmics or ablation when recurrent arrhythmia requires suppression. Review the need for pacing, ATP or CRT before choosing a device system. A successful ablation does not universally remove secondary-prevention risk, although selected tolerated-VT scenarios have disease- and guideline-specific alternatives that differ from this VF survivor. Discuss inappropriate shocks, lead or pocket complications, follow-up, generator replacement and future goals of care. When an event is convincingly reversible, the decision may change, but uncertainty about residual substrate requires individualized review rather than an automatic label of cured. The supplied absence of an acute explanation and good expected survival make device protection appropriate despite EF of forty-two percent.')],['ESC2022','HFUS2022'])

R['ACSUS2025']=('ACC/AHA acute coronary syndromes guideline, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2024.11.009')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Primary-Prevention ICD in Chronic Ischemic Cardiomyopathy',
 'Six months after MI and PCI, a patient remains NYHA class II with LVEF 32% despite chronic maximally tolerated guideline-directed therapy. There has been no sustained ventricular arrhythmia. Meaningful survival beyond one year is expected. Under US guidance, which strategy is appropriate?',
 'Offer an ICD for primary prevention after shared decision-making',[
 ('Wait for sustained VT before offering an ICD','A prior sustained event defines a secondary-prevention pathway. An appropriate primary-prevention indication does not require the patient first to experience that event.'),
 ('Require LVEF at or below thirty percent because all ischemic indications use that threshold','The thirty-percent threshold applies to a specific NYHA class I post-MI recommendation. NYHA II–III patients can qualify with EF at or below thirty-five percent when other criteria are met.'),
 ('Require inducible VT at EPS because no spontaneous arrhythmia has occurred','Selected ischemic NSVT pathways use inducibility, but the supplied EF, symptom and treatment criteria establish a separate recommendation that does not require EPS.')],
 [S('Match EF to Symptoms and Treatment History','**US HF guidance recommends a primary-prevention ICD for eligible ischemic patients with LVEF at most 35%, NYHA II–III symptoms and chronic GDMT.** Meaningful survival beyond one year and the relevant post-MI and revascularization intervals are required.',
    'A distinct recommendation uses LVEF at most 30% with NYHA I symptoms after MI. Do not transfer that stricter EF threshold to every symptomatic patient.',
    'Confirm that EF remains reduced after an adequate treatment period and is measured reliably. An early acute-illness value is not equivalent to persistent chronic dysfunction.'),
  S('Evaluate Device Benefit Within Comprehensive HF Care','**An ICD prevents selected arrhythmic deaths; it does not replace therapies that improve ventricular function and reduce HF events.** Continue appropriate neurohormonal and SGLT2-inhibitor treatment and coronary prevention.',
    'Consider competing mortality, frailty, renal disease and patient goals. A numerical EF criterion does not guarantee net benefit when serious comorbidity limits functional survival.',
    'Review QRS duration, bundle-branch pattern, rhythm and pacing needs to determine whether CRT or another device configuration is also indicated. An ICD indication alone does not automatically establish CRT eligibility. Discuss shocks, infection, lead complications, generator replacement and follow-up. This patient is six months after MI and PCI, so the supplied timing avoids premature prophylactic implantation and allows assessment of persistent dysfunction. New sustained arrhythmia, syncope or clinical deterioration would require a separate urgent assessment rather than waiting for the next routine EF measurement. The purpose of primary prevention is to intervene before a malignant event in a group with demonstrated benefit, not to wait for a monitor to document the event one hopes to prevent.')],['HFUS2022','ACSUS2025'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Timing of Prophylactic ICD Assessment After MI',
 'Twelve days after a first MI treated with PCI, a patient has LVEF 28% and is beginning HF therapy. There has been no sustained VT/VF, unexplained arrhythmic syncope or permanent pacing requirement. Which plan best follows the standard US primary-prevention pathway?',
 'Optimize treatment and reassess EF after the required post-MI and postrevascularization recovery intervals',[
 ('Implant an ICD immediately because EF is below thirty percent','Low EF shortly after MI identifies risk but does not establish benefit from routine immediate prophylactic implantation. Early recovery and competing nonarrhythmic risk affect the evidence.'),
 ('Implant once forty days have elapsed even if the patient remains within ninety days of PCI','The post-MI and postrevascularization intervals address different aspects of recovery. Meeting only the forty-day condition does not automatically complete the standard pathway after recent revascularization.'),
 ('Exclude future ICD eligibility if no arrhythmia occurs during this admission','Primary prevention does not require a sustained inpatient event. Persistent qualifying dysfunction and symptoms after treatment can establish eligibility later even if monitoring remains free of VT.')],
 [S('Allow Recovery Before a Routine Primary-Prevention Decision','**The standard US ischemic primary-prevention framework uses at least 40 days after MI and at least 90 days after revascularization.** Reassess ventricular function and symptoms after treatment rather than permanently classifying the patient from an early EF.',
    'The 2025 US ACS guideline reiterates these intervals for selected prophylactic ICD candidates. EF can improve after reperfusion and GDMT, changing the need for implantation.',
    'The intervals are not permission to leave symptoms, HF or recurrent arrhythmia untreated. Arrange follow-up and a definite reassessment date so a justified delay does not become loss to care.'),
  S('Keep Important Exceptions Separate','**Secondary-prevention events and necessary pacing create different clinical questions.** The stem excludes those circumstances so the routine prophylactic pathway is the appropriate comparison.',
    'Documented late or otherwise nonreversible sustained ventricular arrhythmia needs specialist assessment and should not be dismissed by quoting a primary-prevention waiting period. A clearly transient early ischemic event also requires careful cause assessment.',
    'Wearable defibrillation may be considered selectively as temporary protection, but it is not a universally proven substitute for every waiting-period patient. Continue evidence-based HF and coronary care, review adherence and renal/electrolyte status and escalate new syncope or sustained palpitations promptly. Early-implant trials did not demonstrate an overall survival benefit despite reducing arrhythmic deaths, illustrating why risk alone does not establish benefit from the proposed intervention. Explain that the plan is active treatment with scheduled reassessment, not a judgment that the low EF is harmless. Final implantation depends on persistent criteria, expected meaningful survival and informed patient preference.')],['HFUS2022','ACSUS2025','ESC2022'])

R['IRIS']=('IRIS early post-MI ICD randomized trial, 2009','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa0901889')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Early Post-MI ICD Trials and Competing Mortality',
 'Which interpretation of DINAMIT and IRIS best explains why high early post-MI sudden-death risk does not by itself justify routine immediate prophylactic ICD implantation?',
 'Early ICD implantation reduced arrhythmic or sudden deaths but did not improve overall survival in the studied populations',[
 ('The devices failed to terminate ventricular arrhythmias in the early post-MI period','Lack of an overall survival advantage does not mean the devices had no antiarrhythmic efficacy. Cause-specific deaths and total mortality must be distinguished.'),
 ('The trials established improved overall survival but poor cost-effectiveness','The limitation was not simply economic. The randomized early-implant strategies did not demonstrate the overall survival benefit required to support routine prophylactic use in those populations.'),
 ('The results exclude an ICD indication for any later post-MI survivor','The timing and selection differ from chronic primary-prevention trials and secondary-prevention indications. An early negative trial does not invalidate later implantation when established criteria are met.')],
 [S('Risk Prediction Is Not Proof of Intervention Benefit','**DINAMIT and IRIS evaluated prophylactic ICD implantation early after MI in selected high-risk patients.** Both failed to demonstrate an overall survival advantage despite fewer arrhythmic or sudden deaths.',
    'Nonarrhythmic deaths were more frequent in the ICD groups, offsetting the cause-specific reduction. This illustrates competing risk; it does not establish that each prevented arrhythmic death was causally converted into another type of death.',
    'A device can interrupt VT/VF without preventing progressive pump failure, recurrent ischemic injury or other major causes of mortality during early recovery.'),
  S('Apply the Findings to the Correct Decision','**Routine primary-prevention timing permits treatment and reassessment before selecting persistent-risk patients.** The standard US framework includes at least forty days after MI and ninety days after revascularization when applicable.',
    'Continue active HF and coronary treatment during recovery, and reassess EF and symptoms rather than treating the waiting interval as a period without care. Temporary protection is a separate individualized question.',
    'Do not use these trials to deny secondary-prevention assessment after a documented nonreversible malignant arrhythmia or to ignore necessary pacing. Conversely, a low early EF alone does not prove that immediate implantation improves survival. Evaluate the actual population, comparator and endpoint before transferring trial findings into an examination answer. Explain to patients that high risk can coexist with uncertainty or lack of benefit from a particular intervention at a particular time. Later persistent dysfunction may support an ICD after recovery, while substantial improvement can remove the original primary-prevention rationale. Meaningful survival, competing comorbidity and patient preference remain relevant at the reassessment.')],['IRIS','ACSUS2025','HFUS2022'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Primary-Prevention ICD in Nonischemic DCM',
 'A 56-year-old with nonischemic DCM remains NYHA II with LVEF 30% after six months of maximally tolerated GDMT. There is no transient cause, QRS is narrow and meaningful survival beyond one year is expected. Under US 2022 HF guidance, which plan is appropriate?',
 'Offer primary-prevention ICD treatment after individualized discussion',[
 ('Exclude ICD because the cardiomyopathy is nonischemic','US guidance includes eligible nonischemic DCM with EF at or below thirty-five percent, NYHA II–III symptoms and chronic GDMT. Ischemic etiology is not required for this recommendation.'),
 ('Require a broad QRS before considering an ICD','QRS duration influences CRT selection, not every ICD indication. A narrow QRS does not negate the supplied primary-prevention criteria.'),
 ('Require documented sustained VT before considering any device','That would convert a primary-prevention decision into waiting for a secondary-prevention event. Persistent qualifying dysfunction and symptoms can support implantation without prior sustained VT.')],
 [S('Use the Explicit Guideline Framework','**US 2022 HF guidance recommends ICD treatment in eligible nonischemic DCM with LVEF at most 35%, NYHA II–III symptoms and chronic GDMT when meaningful survival beyond one year is expected.** Confirm that the dysfunction persists after adequate treatment.',
    'Assess reversible causes, including tachycardia-mediated or toxic disease, and investigate etiology with appropriate imaging and genetic evaluation. A disease-specific high-risk genotype or scar phenotype can influence decisions beyond a single EF threshold.',
    'The six-month treated history distinguishes this case from a newly diagnosed patient whose EF may improve promptly with therapy. Narrow QRS means there is no supplied conventional CRT indication, not that defibrillation protection is ineffective.'),
  S('Discuss Heterogeneous Benefit and Competing Risks','**Nonischemic DCM is a diverse group, and trial findings must be interpreted alongside age, substrate and competing mortality.** DANISH did not show a significant overall all-cause mortality reduction in its studied population, while reducing sudden death.',
    'This result does not erase the named US recommendation or prove identical benefit for every patient. European recommendation strength and disease-specific risk approaches differ, so identify the framework when writing or answering a question.',
    'Continue comprehensive HF treatment and reassess symptoms, renal function and expected survival. Discuss inappropriate shocks, infection, lead complications and future generator changes rather than presenting implantation as a purely numerical decision. Severe comorbidity limiting survival can change the balance despite a qualifying EF. Incorporate the patient’s goals and consider advanced HF options when appropriate. A later EF improvement, new arrhythmia or newly identified genetic diagnosis can alter follow-up decisions, but should be evaluated from current evidence rather than an assumption that every nonischemic heart behaves alike.')],['HFUS2022','CMP2023','ESC2022'])

R['DANISH']=('DANISH randomized trial, 2016','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1608029')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Interpreting DANISH Without Overstating a Negative Endpoint',
 'Which statement accurately summarizes the original DANISH trial of prophylactic ICD therapy in nonischemic systolic HF?',
 'ICD treatment reduced sudden cardiac death but did not significantly reduce all-cause mortality in the overall trial population',[
 ('ICD treatment significantly reduced all-cause mortality without reducing sudden death','This reverses the trial’s findings. Sudden death was reduced, while the overall all-cause mortality primary endpoint was not significantly lower.'),
 ('ICD treatment and control were proven equivalent for survival','Failure to demonstrate superiority is not an equivalence result. The confidence interval includes different plausible effects and must be interpreted with trial design and population.'),
 ('The study compared CRT-defibrillators with medical treatment that excluded CRT','CRT was used in a substantial proportion of both groups when indicated. The comparison tested added defibrillator protection on contemporary background care rather than CRT versus its absence.')],
 [S('Keep the Primary and Secondary Outcomes Distinct','**DANISH enrolled 1116 patients with nonischemic systolic HF and LVEF at most 35%.** Patients received an ICD or usual care, with CRT in approximately 58% of each group.',
    'At median follow-up of 67.6 months, all-cause mortality was 21.6% with ICD versus 23.4% in controls: HR 0.87, 95% CI 0.68–1.12. This did not establish a significant reduction in the primary endpoint.',
    'Sudden cardiac death was 4.3% versus 8.2%, HR 0.50, 95% CI 0.31–0.82. The cause-specific benefit should not disappear from the interpretation merely because the primary endpoint was negative.'),
  S('Translate the Trial Through Patient Selection','**Competing nonarrhythmic mortality affects the potential overall benefit of defibrillator protection.** Age, comorbidity, HF severity and contemporary treatment influence the proportion of deaths an ICD can prevent.',
    'Age-related analyses inform discussion but do not create a universal age prohibition. Subgroup or extended-follow-up findings require their own attribution rather than silently replacing the original randomized primary result.',
    'The trial does not prove absence of benefit in every nonischemic phenotype or invalidate all guideline recommendations. Genetic risk, myocardial scar and arrhythmic history can identify patients unlike the average trial participant. Conversely, an EF threshold should not override severe competing disease that limits meaningful survival. Discuss procedural and long-term device harms as well as potential prevention of sudden death, and continue GDMT and indicated CRT. A statistically nonsignificant all-cause result is neither proof of equivalence nor evidence that the device cannot terminate malignant arrhythmia. Explicitly name the outcome when describing benefit so patients and examinees do not confuse sudden-death prevention with a guaranteed overall survival extension.')],['DANISH','HFUS2022','CMP2023'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'DCM Risk Assessment Above the Conventional EF Threshold',
 'A patient with DCM has LVEF 44% despite treatment, unexplained syncope and significant LGE on CMR. No sustained VT has been documented. Under the ESC 2022 multiparametric framework, which next approach is most appropriate?',
 'Consider primary-prevention ICD assessment because EF below fifty percent and two specified risk factors are present',[
 ('Exclude ICD assessment because EF is above thirty-five percent','The conventional EF threshold does not encompass every high-risk DCM phenotype. ESC incorporates additional clinical, imaging and genetic evidence in selected patients with EF below fifty percent.'),
 ('Treat LGE alone as a mandatory ICD indication regardless of the rest of the phenotype','Scar contributes to risk, but its presence is not a universal standalone mandate across all diseases and burdens. This question supplies a second relevant feature and a specified DCM framework.'),
 ('Require a pathogenic variant before clinical and imaging risk factors can be used','Genetics can materially affect assessment, but unexplained syncope and LGE are themselves included risk factors. A pathogenic molecular result is not required for their combination to be relevant.')],
 [S('Integrate the Risk Features Rather Than EF Alone','**ESC 2022 advises considering an ICD in DCM or hypokinetic nondilated cardiomyopathy with LVEF below 50% and at least two specified risk factors.** Unexplained syncope and LGE on CMR satisfy two features in this stem.',
    'The framework also includes inducible sustained monomorphic VT and selected pathogenic variants, particularly PLN, FLNC or RBM20. LMNA-associated disease additionally has a gene-specific risk-calculation pathway.',
    'Confirm that the syncope remains unexplained after appropriate assessment and that LGE is reliably interpreted. Nonspecific dizziness or an imaging artefact should not be promoted into a qualifying risk factor.'),
  S('Recognize Disease-Specific and Genetic Nuance','**The 2023 cardiomyopathy guideline further emphasizes high-risk genetic backgrounds and scar phenotypes that can justify assessment above an EF of 35%.** Variant type, expression, arrhythmia and family history affect interpretation.',
    'For LMNA disease, use the validated risk framework with its clinical phenotype requirements rather than counting a gene name as an automatic device mandate. A variant of uncertain significance is not a pathogenic-risk criterion.',
    'A recommendation to consider treatment requires shared assessment of absolute risk, competing mortality, expected survival and device complications. It is not the same as a class I instruction for every mildly reduced EF. Continue HF care, investigate the underlying disease and arrange familial assessment where indicated. Additional rhythm monitoring or EPS can answer selected unresolved questions, but should be chosen for a defined purpose. The reason to refer this patient is the converging clinical and scar evidence within a named framework, not a claim that every LGE-positive heart needs an ICD. Reassess as ventricular function, arrhythmia burden and the molecular diagnosis evolve.')],['ESC2022','CMP2023'])

R['ICDAUC2025']=('Multisociety ICD/CRT/pacing appropriate use criteria, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2024.11.023')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Subcutaneous Versus Transvenous ICD Capabilities',
 'An ICD candidate has recurrent monomorphic VT reliably terminated by ATP and also needs ongoing bradycardia pacing. Which device-selection statement is most appropriate?',
 'A conventional standalone subcutaneous ICD does not provide the required ATP and chronic pacing functions',[
 ('A standalone subcutaneous ICD provides equivalent chronic bradycardia pacing','Brief postshock pacing capability is not equivalent to dependable chronic bradycardia support. The required function must be assessed before choosing the system.'),
 ('A standalone subcutaneous ICD supplies routine ATP for monomorphic VT','Conventional standalone S-ICD systems do not provide ATP. A system capable of pacing-mediated termination is relevant when this repeatedly useful therapy is needed.'),
 ('Avoiding an intravascular lead removes the need to assess pacing requirements','Extravascular placement can reduce particular lead-related burdens, but does not make missing therapeutic capabilities irrelevant. Device selection must address both defibrillation and pacing needs.')],
 [S('Select the System for the Required Therapies','**A conventional standalone S-ICD provides defibrillation without routine ATP, chronic bradycardia pacing or CRT.** It can be appropriate when those functions are neither needed nor anticipated.',
    'Transvenous systems can provide bradycardia pacing and ATP, with CRT-defibrillator configurations when resynchronization is indicated. Not every transvenous ICD automatically provides CRT.',
    'The repeated ATP-responsive rhythm and existing bradycardia requirement make a standalone S-ICD a poor match here, irrespective of whether it can deliver an effective shock.'),
  S('Balance Capabilities Against Long-Term Burden','**Subcutaneous placement avoids an intravascular defibrillator lead but retains pocket, sensing and shock-related risks.** Assess ECG screening, anatomy, infection history, vascular access and likely future pacing needs.',
    'Newer substernal extravascular ICDs and modular combinations with leadless pacing are distinct systems. Their capabilities and availability must not be conflated with a conventional standalone S-ICD.',
    'An independent pacemaker or modular approach may solve selected problems, but adds compatibility, follow-up and procedural considerations rather than making all systems interchangeable. Discuss ATP benefit, potential acceleration, shocks, lead failure and extraction implications. A younger patient without pacing needs may prioritize avoiding intravascular hardware, while a patient with recurrent slow monomorphic VT may derive substantial benefit from ATP. Reassess the expected disease course and preferences before implantation. The correct question is which system can deliver the patient’s required therapies with an acceptable burden, not which device is newest or has the fewest leads.')],['ICDAUC2025','ESC2022'])

R['MADITRIT']=('MADIT-RIT randomized programming trial, 2012','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1211107')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Programming to Reduce Avoidable ICD Therapy',
 'A primary-prevention ICD patient has therapies for short self-terminating episodes and rapidly conducted atrial arrhythmia. There is no known clinically important slow VT. Which programming review is most appropriate?',
 'Review longer detection, appropriate rate thresholds, discrimination and ATP settings for the documented rhythms',[
 ('Shorten detection intervals to treat every brief fast rhythm sooner','Short detection can increase therapy for rhythms that would terminate spontaneously. Faster treatment is necessary for some dangerous rhythms, but is not a universal improvement.'),
 ('Disable ATP so every detected ventricular episode receives a shock','ATP can painlessly terminate selected monomorphic VT. Removing it indiscriminately can increase shock burden without correcting inappropriate detection.'),
 ('Raise the therapy threshold without reviewing stored rhythms or prior clinical VT','Higher cut-offs can reduce avoidable therapy in suitable populations, but can leave documented slower VT untreated. Programming must account for the actual clinical rhythm rather than applying one threshold blindly.')],
 [S('Detection Is a Treatment Decision','**Rate and duration thresholds determine which episodes receive therapy.** Longer detection can permit nonsustained arrhythmia to stop, while discrimination can reduce treatment of supraventricular rhythms.',
    'Review stored electrograms to identify the actual reason for therapy. AF, other SVT, oversensing and true ventricular episodes require different corrective actions.',
    'ATP can terminate monomorphic reentry without a painful shock, but can occasionally accelerate VT. Program sequences and shock backup according to rhythm characteristics and the device.'),
  S('Apply Trial Evidence Without Ignoring Clinical VT','**MADIT-RIT tested high-rate or delayed-therapy programming against conventional programming in primary-prevention recipients.** It showed substantially less inappropriate therapy; the high-rate strategy also significantly reduced all-cause mortality in the original comparison.',
    'This does not justify leaving known slower symptomatic VT outside detection zones. Secondary-prevention histories and drug-induced slowing of VT can require different settings.',
    'Programming reduces unnecessary interventions but does not cure AF, repair a fractured lead or remove ventricular scar. Treat those problems in parallel. A monitor-only zone can help characterize selected rhythms, but should not replace treatment of a known dangerous episode. Review symptoms, syncope, therapies and remote monitoring after changes, and explain what the patient should do after shocks. Avoid interpreting a lower shock count as proof of absent arrhythmia without examining detection and episode data. The aim is dependable treatment of clinically consequential rhythms with fewer avoidable therapies, not simply minimizing a device counter at the expense of safety.')],['MADITRIT','STORM2024','ICDAUC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Recurrent ICD Shocks From Oversensing',
 'An alert patient receives recurrent ICD shocks while simultaneous monitored rhythm remains sinus at 82/min. Interrogation shows nonphysiological high-frequency ventricular signals consistent with lead noise. Which immediate strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Suspend inappropriate tachytherapies under monitoring with external rescue available and arrange urgent device-system assessment',[
 ('Load amiodarone to suppress the presumed ventricular storm','Antiarrhythmics can suppress true recurrent ventricular arrhythmias, but the recorded problem is false detection from lead noise. Drug loading does not repair the sensing fault and can add toxicity.'),
 ('Leave therapies active until a lead procedure can be completed','Continued inappropriate shocks cause pain and can be dangerous. Temporary inhibition with reliable monitoring and external defibrillation support addresses the immediate harm while definitive correction is organized.'),
 ('Apply a magnet and discharge once the shocks stop','A magnet may temporarily inhibit ICD tachytherapies, but does not resolve the lead fault. Response is device-specific, and inhibited protection requires supervised rescue capability and a definitive plan.')],
 [S('Establish Whether Therapy Was Appropriate','**Stored electrograms and simultaneous clinical monitoring distinguish genuine ventricular arrhythmia from oversensing.** Lead noise, T-wave oversensing, electromagnetic interference and supraventricular rhythms can produce inappropriate detection.',
    'A patient can remain conscious during real VT, so alertness alone does not establish inappropriate therapy. Here sinus rhythm during shocks and nonphysiological stored signals supply the stronger evidence.',
    'Inspect sensing and impedance trends, lead integrity and relevant imaging or provoking circumstances through the device team. A normal single impedance value does not exclude an intermittent lead problem.'),
  S('Stop Harm While Maintaining Rescue','**Temporarily inhibit inappropriate ICD therapies with programming or an appropriate magnet response while maintaining continuous monitoring and external defibrillation readiness.** Do not confuse therapy inhibition with correction of the cause.',
    'An ICD magnet generally affects tachyarrhythmia detection or treatment rather than converting bradycardia pacing to an asynchronous mode. Manufacturer, model, programmed response and magnet position must be verified.',
    'Arrange urgent electrophysiological/device-system review and the necessary lead or programming intervention. Reassess the patient after repeated shocks and provide analgesia or sedation when appropriate. If genuine VT/VF develops while internal therapies are disabled, external rescue must be immediately available. Restore a verified protective plan before leaving monitored care, and communicate any temporary settings to the receiving team. This differs from appropriate shocks during electrical storm, where sympathetic treatment, antiarrhythmics and ablation can be central. The first therapeutic distinction is whether the device is treating the patient’s arrhythmia or responding to a false signal; the same shock count can represent very different emergencies.')],['STORM2024','ICDAUC2025'])

R['VEST']=('VEST randomized wearable-defibrillator trial, 2018','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1800781')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Wearable Defibrillator Evidence After MI',
 'A patient with LVEF 30% soon after MI asks whether VEST proved that every patient awaiting primary-prevention reassessment should receive a wearable defibrillator. Which response is most accurate?',
 'VEST did not significantly reduce its primary arrhythmic-death endpoint in intention-to-treat analysis; temporary use requires individual assessment',[
 ('VEST established a significant primary-endpoint benefit requiring universal post-MI use','The primary arrhythmic-death comparison was not statistically significant. Selected temporary use should not be represented as a universal mandate derived from that trial.'),
 ('VEST proved that a wearable device cannot terminate ventricular arrhythmia','A negative primary outcome does not mean the device cannot deliver an effective shock. Clinical effectiveness also depends on wear time, patient selection and competing causes of death.'),
 ('A secondary all-cause mortality finding establishes the primary arrhythmic endpoint as positive','Primary and secondary endpoints must remain distinct. An uncorrected secondary signal cannot be substituted for the prespecified primary result.')],
 [S('Read the Prespecified Outcome and Treatment Exposure','**VEST randomized recent-MI patients with LVEF at most 35% to a wearable defibrillator plus medical treatment or medical treatment alone.** The main question concerned arrhythmic death during the early period before routine ICD eligibility.',
    'Arrhythmic death occurred in 1.6% versus 2.4%, relative risk 0.67 with a 95% CI of 0.37–1.21. The primary intention-to-treat result was not statistically significant.',
    'Median wear time was approximately eighteen hours daily and declined over follow-up. A device cannot provide protection while unworn, but adherence-based analyses are vulnerable to selection and cannot simply replace randomization.'),
  S('Use Temporary Protection for a Defined Clinical Problem','**A wearable defibrillator can be considered selectively when transient risk or a temporary barrier to an indicated implanted device makes bridging useful.** Examples such as device extraction and early post-MI assessment have different evidence and circumstances.',
    'It does not provide chronic pacing, ATP or CRT. Assess the patient’s ability and willingness to wear it, alarm handling, skin problems, inappropriate therapy risk and access to follow-up.',
    'Continue GDMT and schedule EF and clinical reassessment rather than allowing a temporary device to postpone a durable decision indefinitely. An indicated ICD should not be replaced by indefinite wearable use solely because the vest can deliver shocks. Conversely, lack of a significant primary VEST result is not proof of no possible benefit in any selected bridging situation. Explain the trial’s uncertainty and the individual rationale explicitly. Review the date and purpose of reassessment, because recovery may remove a prophylactic indication while persistent qualifying dysfunction can support implantation. Device use supplements rather than replaces coronary treatment, HF optimization and prompt assessment of new syncope or sustained arrhythmia.')],['VEST','ESC2022','ICDAUC2025'],style='direct')

R['SUD2020']=('APHRS/HRS unexplained sudden death and family investigation consensus, 2020','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.hrthm.2020.10.010')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Family Evaluation After Unexplained Young Sudden Death',
 'A 29-year-old dies suddenly during sleep. Expert autopsy and toxicology find no cause. The asymptomatic parents and siblings ask whether a normal routine ECG is sufficient to end investigation. Which plan is most appropriate?',
 'Arrange multidisciplinary first-degree-relative screening and review of retained postmortem material, with phenotype-directed genetic counseling',[
 ('End family evaluation because a negative autopsy excludes inherited cardiac disease','Primary electrical disorders may leave no structural autopsy finding, and early cardiomyopathy can be subtle. A negative autopsy does not establish absence of inherited susceptibility.'),
 ('Use a broad genetic panel in each relative as a replacement for clinical screening','Unfocused testing can produce uncertain variants without identifying the cause. Clinical phenotype assessment and appropriate analysis of the decedent or an affected relative guide more interpretable testing.'),
 ('Discharge all relatives permanently after one normal resting ECG','A single ECG cannot exclude all concealed or age-dependent phenotypes. Initial screening uses complementary tests, and selected periodic re-evaluation may be needed when the cause remains unknown.')],
 [S('Investigate the Death and the Family Together','**Unexplained sudden death at a young age warrants expert review of the circumstances, autopsy, toxicology and available premortem records.** Retain suitable blood or tissue for postmortem molecular investigation when possible.',
    'Obtain a three-generation pedigree, including unexplained drowning, seizure-like events, syncope, cardiomyopathy and premature pacemaker implantation. A death during sleep can be relevant but does not uniquely diagnose Brugada syndrome.',
    'The 2020 APHRS/HRS consensus advises first-degree-relative screening after negative or absent autopsy when the decedent was younger than forty-five years, and with a clear phenotype regardless of age.'),
  S('Use Clinical Screening and Genetics With Appropriate Interpretation','**Initial evaluation can include history, examination, ECG with high right-precordial leads, echocardiography and exercise testing.** Ambulatory monitoring, CMR and pharmacological provocation are selected according to the findings and specialist assessment.',
    'When a pathogenic or likely pathogenic familial cause is identified, offer appropriately counseled cascade testing. A variant of uncertain significance must not be used as proof that a healthy relative has inherited a lethal disorder.',
    'If no phenotype or causal result emerges, consider repeat clinical assessment rather than indiscriminate repeated broad genetic panels. The consensus recommends reassessment approximately every three to five years until at least age forty-five in selected unexplained young-death families, with timing adjusted for new symptoms or multiple events. A normal first screen is reassuring evidence, not a guarantee against later expression. Provide bereavement and psychological support alongside clear guidance on symptoms that require prompt review. Prevention decisions follow the identified disease and individual risk; family history alone does not automatically require an ICD in every relative. Preserve records and revisit variant interpretation or the pedigree when new evidence appears, because a later diagnosis in one member can clarify care for the entire family.')],['SUD2020','ESC2022','GENETICS2022'])

if __name__=='__main__':
    decisions=[(t,d)for t,items in SECTIONS for d in items]
    assert len(rows)==len(decisions)
    for r,(t,d)in zip(rows,decisions):assert r['topic']==t;r['coverageDecision']=d
    p=save('Cardiology',7,DOC,rows,R,'Original postgraduate educational synthesis with source checks and author editorial review. Full Harrison text not accessed; independent clinician review not claimed.')
    b=json.loads(p.read_text(encoding='utf-8'));b.update(status='chapter_authored',plannedQuestions=len(decisions));p.write_text(json.dumps(b,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
